              "rationale": "Phases of Wound Healing: (1) Hemostasis & Coagulation (Minutes to Hours): Platelet plug, fibrin clot, release of PDGF and TGF-beta; (2) Inflammatory Phase (Days 1 to 4): Neutrophils arrive first (peak at 24-48 hrs, decontaminate bacteria), followed by Macrophages (peak at 48-72 hrs; macrophages are the MASTER REGULATORS of wound healing, essential for transition to repair); (3) Proliferative Phase (Days 4 to 14): Fibroblast migration, angiogenesis, and synthesis of Granulation Tissue with predominantly TYPE III COLLAGEN; (4) Remodeling / Maturation Phase (Day 21 up to 1-2 years): Type III collagen is degraded by collagenases and replaced by stronger, highly organized TYPE I COLLAGEN cross-linked by lysyl oxidase. Tensile strength increases fastest between weeks 3 and 6. By 3 months, scar tensile strength plateaus at a MAXIMUM OF 70-80% of normal uninjured skin—a scar NEVER regains 100% of original tensile strength!",
