              "rationale": "Keloid vs Hypertrophic Scar Comparison: (1) Keloid: Exuberant overgrowth of dense fibrotic tissue that EXTENDS BEYOND THE BOUNDARIES of the original incisional wound or trauma, invading into normal adjacent skin. High prevalence in individuals of African and Asian ancestry (genetic predisposition). Predilection sites: Earlobe (ear piercing), presternal chest, shoulders, upper back. Does NOT spontaneously regress with time. High rate of recurrence (> 50-80%) after simple surgical excision! Histopathology: Abundant, thick, glassy, disorganized hyalinized 'Keloidal' collagen bundles (composed predominantly of Type I and Type III collagen). First-line treatment: Intralesional Triamcinolone Acetonide injection +/- silicone gel sheeting or pulsed dye laser. (2) Hypertrophic Scar: Remains STRICTLY CONFINED WITHIN the original wound margins. Often develops in wounds crossing relaxed skin tension lines (Langer's lines) or areas of excessive wound tension / delayed healing. Often spontaneously regresses/flattens over 1-2 years. Histopathology: Parallel, organized arrays of fine collagen fibers (predominantly Type III collagen).",
