              "rationale": "Flail Chest is defined anatomically as the fracture of >= 3 contiguous ribs in >= 2 places each, producing a detached, free-floating thoracic wall segment. Hallmark: PARADOXICAL RESPIRATORY MOTION: During inspiration, negative intrathoracic pressure pulls the flail segment INWARD; during expiration, positive intrathoracic pressure pushes the segment OUTWARD. While paradoxical motion impairs mechanics, the primary pathophysiology driving severe, life-threatening arterial hypoxemia is the UNDERLYING PULMONARY CONTUSION (alveolar-capillary membrane disruption, microvascular hemorrhage, alveolar flooding, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch) beneath the crushed ribs. Modern management: Aggressive multimodal analgesia (Thoracic Epidural or Erector Spinae Plane block), meticulous pulmonary toilet, fluid restriction (prevent fluid overload in wet lung), and selective non-invasive or invasive mechanical ventilation.",
