              "rationale": "Cushing's Triad is a late, physiological response indicating severe, life-threatening intracranial hypertension (raised ICP) and brainstem ischemia with imminent brain herniation. The Triad consists of: (1) Systemic Hypertension with Widened Pulse Pressure (reflex sympathetic response to overcome elevated ICP and restore cerebral perfusion pressure: CPP = MAP - ICP), (2) Bradycardia (baroreceptor response in carotid sinus and aortic arch triggered by severe hypertension, mediated by vagal parasympathetic outflow), and (3) Irregular, Depressed Respirations (compression and distortion of the respiratory pacemaker centers in the medulla and pons, leading to Cheyne-Stokes breathing or ataxic respirations). Immediate interventions to lower ICP: Elevate head of bed 30 degrees (promotes jugular venous drainage), Hyperventilation to target PaCO2 30-35 mmHg (causes cerebral vasoconstriction; use only for brief acute herniation crises!), IV Osmotherapy (20% Mannitol 0.5-1 g/kg or 3% Hypertonic Saline bolus), and emergent decompressive craniectomy.",
