              "rationale": "Small vs Large Bowel Obstruction Comparison: (1) Small Bowel Obstruction (SBO): Most common cause overall is POSTOPERATIVE PERITONEAL ADHESIONS (60-75%), followed by Incarcerated Hernias (15%) and Malignancy (5%). Presentation: Early, frequent, voluminous, bile-stained vomiting, severe cramping central abdominal colic, and moderate distension. Plain Radiograph: Centrally located dilated loops (> 3 cm diameter) arranged in a 'step-ladder' pattern, with VALVULAE CONNIVENTES (Plicae Circulares / Kerckring folds), which are fine, closely spaced mucosal folds that completely cross the FULL TRANVERSE WIDTH of the bowel lumen from wall to wall ('String of pearls / String of beads' sign on CT). (2) Large Bowel Obstruction (LBO): Most common cause is COLORECTAL ADENOCARCINOMA (60-70%), followed by Diverticulitis (10%) and Sigmoid Volvulus (10-15%). Presentation: Marked, massive abdominal distension, late or absent vomiting, and early complete constipation (obstipation). Plain Radiograph: Peripherally located distended loops (> 6 cm colon, > 9 cm cecum), with HAUSTRA (haustral folds do NOT cross the full luminal width, separated by taenia coli). Incompetent ileocecal valve converts LBO into an open loop; Competent ileocecal valve creates a high-pressure CLOSED-LOOP OBSTRUCTION with extreme risk of cecal gangrene and perforation (Laplace's Law: tension is greatest where diameter is largest -> Cecum perforates first!).",
