            "topic": "Hirschsprung's Disease - Aganglionosis & Biopsy",
            "question": "A full-term male newborn fails to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life and develops abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. Rectal digital examination demonstrates a tight anal sphincter with an explosive, forceful expulsion of liquid feces and gas upon withdrawal of the finger ('Blast Sign' / Squirt sign). Barium enema reveals a narrow, spastic distal segment with proximal funnel-shaped transition zone into a massively dilated colon. What is the gold-standard diagnostic investigation, and what histological absence confirms Hirschsprung's disease?",
