              "rationale": "Hirschsprung's Disease (Congenital Aganglionic Megacolon) occurs in 1 in 5,000 live births (4:1 male predominance; 10-15% associated with Down Syndrome). Pathophysiology: Arrest of craniocaudal migration of Neural Crest Cells along the gastrointestinal tract during weeks 5 to 12 of gestation. The distal bowel segment (rectosigmoid colon in 75-80% - short-segment Hirschsprung) fails to develop enteric ganglion cells. Because enteric ganglion cells are inhibitory (releasing NO and VIP), the aganglionic segment is tonically contracted and spastic, creating functional obstruction with secondary massive dilation of the proximal normal ganglionic bowel ('Megacolon'). Clinical Signs: Failure to pass meconium in first 48 hours (> 90%), abdominal distension, bilious emesis, and 'Blast sign' on rectal exam. GOLD-STANDARD CONFIRMATORY TEST: RECTAL SUCTION BIOPSY taken at >= 2 cm above the dentate line (avoiding physiological hypoganglionosis of the anal canal). Histopathology confirms: (1) Complete ABSENCE of ganglion cells in the submucosal (Meissner's) and myenteric (Auerbach's) plexuses, and (2) Hypertrophy of non-myelinated acetylcholinesterase-positive nerve fibers. Surgical cures: Pull-through procedures (Swenson, Duhamel, or Soave endorectal pull-through).",
