              "rationale": "Colorectal Carcinoma (CRC) Right vs Left Sided Comparison: (1) Right-Sided (Proximal / Cecum and Ascending Colon): Because the cecum is wide and distensible and stool is liquid, tumors grow to large sizes as EXOPHYTIC, FUNGATING, polypoid, friable masses before causing obstruction. Hallmark Presentation: Insidious occult gastrointestinal bleeding, microcytic Iron Deficiency Anemia (fatigue, pallor, weakness), weight loss, and occasionally a palpable right iliac fossa mass. ('Right = Bleeds'). Rule: ANY elderly male or postmenopausal female with unexplained iron-deficiency anemia has cecal cancer until proven otherwise! (2) Left-Sided (Distal / Descending and Sigmoid Colon): The descending and sigmoid colon has a narrower lumen and stool is semi-solid or solid. Tumors grow circumferentially as INFILTRATING, ANNULAR, stenosing lesions ('Napkin-Ring' or 'Apple-Core' lesion on barium enema). Hallmark Presentation: Early alteration in bowel habits (alternating constipation and diarrhea), pencil-thin / ribbon-like stools, hematochezia (bright red blood mixed with stool), and mechanical bowel obstruction ('Left = Obstructs'). Rectal Cancer: Tenesmus (painful incomplete evacuation), hematochezia, and mucus discharge.",
