              "rationale": "Anal Fissure is a longitudinal tear in the anoderm distal to the dentate line. Etiology: Passage of hard, dry stool or explosive diarrhea. Pathophysiology: Spasm of the Internal Anal Sphincter generates high resting anal canal pressure, causing severe local ischemia at the POSTERIOR MIDLINE (which has relatively poor baseline blood perfusion). Over 90% of primary anal fissures occur in the POSTERIOR MIDLINE; ~10% occur in the Anterior Midline (more common in females after vaginal delivery). Warning Rule: Lateral anal fissures are atypical and suggest underlying systemic disease: Crohn's disease, Tuberculosis, HIV, Syphilis, or Anal Cancer. Chronic Anal Fissure Triad: (1) Deep fissure displaying transverse internal sphincter fibers at base, (2) Sentinel skin tag at distal margin, and (3) Hypertrophied anal papilla at proximal margin. Medical Management: High-fiber diet, stool softeners, warm sitz baths, and Chemical Sphincterotomy: Topical Nitroglycerin (0.2-0.4%) or Topical Diltiazem (2%) ointment, or Botulinum toxin injection. Gold-Standard Surgical Operation: LATERAL INTERNAL SPHINCTEROTOMY (LIS: division of the lower 1/3 of the internal anal sphincter up to the dentate line; achieves > 95% cure rate).",
