              "rationale": "Gastric Polyps Classification: (1) Fundic Gland Polyps (FGPs): The most common gastric polyp in Western and urban populations (accounting for > 50-70% of all gastric polyps). Strongly associated with chronic long-term PROTON PUMP INHIBITOR (PPI) use (PPI inhibition of acid causes hypergastrinemia, stimulating parietal cell hyperplasia and cystic glandular dilatation). Located strictly in the gastric acid-producing territory (fundus and body). When sporadic, fundic gland polyps have virtually ZERO risk of malignant transformation (< 0.1%). Note: When multiple FGPs occur in syndromic patients (FAP / Gardner syndrome), low-grade dysplasia may occur. (2) Hyperplastic Polyps: Common in areas with high H. pylori prevalence; arise in setting of chronic active gastritis / mucosal inflammation; low malignant potential (< 1-2%); frequently regress following H. pylori eradication. (3) Gastric Adenomas (Tubular / Villous): True neoplastic polyps; strong association with chronic atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia; carry a HIGH RISK of malignant transformation (up to 30-40% if size > 2 cm); ALL gastric adenomas mandate complete endoscopic polypectomy with surveillance.",
