              "rationale": "Choledochal Cysts are congenital cystic dilatations of the extrahepatic and/or intrahepatic biliary tree (4:1 female predominance). Etiology: Anomalous Pancreaticobiliary Junction (APBJ / APBDJ): the pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct outside the duodenal wall (> 1 cm proximal to the sphincter of Oddi), forming a long common channel that allows reflux of pancreatic enzymes into the biliary tree, causing ductal wall destruction and cystic expansion. Clinical Triad (seen in ~20-30% of children): Abdominal Pain + Jaundice + Palpable RUQ Mass. TODANI CLASSIFICATION: Type I (most common, 80-90%): Fusiform or saccular dilatation of the extrahepatic common bile duct; Type II (2-3%): True diverticulum of extrahepatic duct; Type III (Choledochocele, 2-5%): Intraduodenal cystic dilatation of terminal CBD; Type IV (15-20%): Multiple cysts (IVa: intra- and extrahepatic; IVb: extrahepatic only); Type V (Caroli's Disease): Multiple intrahepatic cystic dilatations (associated with congenital hepatic fibrosis). CRITICAL ONCOLOGICAL HAZARD: Choledochal cysts have a 15-30% lifetime risk of developing CHOLANGIOCARCINOMA due to chronic irritation from refluxed pancreatic juice. Definitive Surgery for Type I and IV: COMPLETE SURGICAL CYST EXCISION with reconstruction via ROUX-EN-Y HEPATICOJEJUNOSTOMY. Simple internal drainage (cystenterostomy) is strictly OBSOLETE and contraindicated because it leaves the premalignant cyst behind!",
