              "rationale": "Amoebic vs Pyogenic Liver Abscess Comparison: (1) Amoebic Liver Abscess: Caused by Entamoeba histolytica (fecal-oral ingestion of cysts). Demographics: Young adult males (9:1 male predominance, ages 20-40). Anatomy: Usually SOLITARY (> 80%), predominantly in the RIGHT LOBE (Segment VII/VIII, due to streaming of superior mesenteric venous flow). Aspirate: Pathognomonic sterile, odorless, thick, reddish-brown fluid resembling 'ANCHOVY SAUCE' or chocolate paste (composed of liquefied necrotic hepatocytes, RBCs, and debris; trophozoites are located in the living peripheral abscess WALL, NOT in the central necrotic fluid!). Serology: Serum Indirect Hemagglutination / ELISA anti-amoebic antibodies positive in > 95%. Treatment: MEDICAL FIRST-LINE: Oral METRONIDAZOLE (750 mg tid for 7-10 days) or Tinidazole, followed by a luminal agent (Diloxanide furoate or Paromomycin) to eradicate asymptomatic colonic carriage. Percutaneous aspiration is indicated ONLY if: large abscess (> 5-10 cm), left lobe abscess (threatens pericardial rupture!), failure to improve after 48-72 hrs of antibiotics, or impending rupture. (2) Pyogenic Liver Abscess: Caused by ascending cholangitis (biliary, 40-50%), pylephlebitis (diverticulitis, appendicitis), or hematogenous spread. Demographics: Older patients, diabetics. Anatomy: Frequently MULTIPLE, bilateral. Pathogens: E. coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae (notorious for gas formation and metastatic endophthalmitis in diabetics), Streptococcus milleri group, anaerobes. Treatment: Percutaneous Catheter Drainage (PCD) under ultrasound/CT guidance + 4-6 weeks of IV antibiotics (Ceftriaxone + Metronidazole).",
