              "rationale": "Hydatid Disease (Cystic Echinococcosis) is caused by the larval stage of the dog tapeworm ECHINOCOCCUS GRANULOSUS. Definitive host: Dog / canines (harbors adult tapeworm in intestine); Intermediate host: Sheep, cattle, humans (accidental dead-end intermediate host via ingestion of eggs from contaminated food/water). Anatomy of Hydatid Cyst Wall: (1) Pericyst (outer adventitial fibrous layer formed by compressed host liver tissue), (2) Ectocyst (middle acellular, laminated, elastic chitinous membrane), and (3) Endocyst (inner germinal layer, cellular, produces daughter cysts and hydatid sand containing hooklets and protoscolices). Imaging Hallmarks: Ultrasound Gharbi / WHO classification: Stage CE1 (unilocular), Stage CE2 (multivesicular with 'Cartwheel' / 'Honeycomb' pattern), Stage CE3 (detached undulating endocyst floating in cavity = 'WATER-LILY SIGN' / sign of Camalote), Stage CE5 (thick calcified dead cyst). DANGEROUS HAZARD: Hydatid cyst fluid contains highly antigenic foreign proteins under high hydrostatic pressure (up to 30-50 cmH2O). Accidental spillage or puncture during surgery/aspiration triggers immediate CATASTROPHIC ANAPHYLACTIC SHOCK and widespread intraperitoneal seeding of scolices (secondary peritoneal echinococcosis). Management: (1) Albendazole (10-15 mg/kg/day started prior to intervention); (2) PAIR Technique (Puncture, Aspiration, Injection of scolicidal agent [hypertonic saline 20% or absolute alcohol; formalin is CONTRAINDICATED due to sclerosing cholangitis risk!], Re-aspiration); (3) Surgery (Laparoscopic or open total cystopericystectomy / capitonnage).",
