            "question": "A 21-year-old college rugby player sustains blunt trauma to his left flank. CT abdomen demonstrates a Grade III splenic laceration with 4 cm parenchymal depth and subcapsular hematoma involving 35% of surface area, with no active contrast extravasation ('blush'). Hemodynamics are stable. Why is Non-Operative Management (NOM) prioritized in splenic trauma, and what lethal encapsulation syndrome occurs after splenectomy?",
