              "rationale": "The Gastrinoma Triangle (Passaro's Triangle, described in 1972) is an anatomical triangle within which > 80-90% of all Gastrinomas (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome) originate. Boundaries of Passaro's Triangle: (1) Superior Apex: Confluence of the Cystic Duct and Common Bile Duct; (2) Inferior Apex: Junction of the Second (descending) and Third (horizontal) parts of the Duodenum; (3) Medial Apex: Junction of the Neck and Body of the Pancreas. Primary gastrinomas inside this triangle arise most commonly within the SUBMUCOSA OF THE DUODENUM (45-50%, often tiny < 5 mm, requires duodenotomy with digital palpation and transillumination) or the HEAD/PROCESS OF THE PANCREAS (30-40%). Preoperative imaging: Gallium-68 DOTATOC / DOTATATE PET-CT and Endoscopic Ultrasound (EUS).",
