              "rationale": "Nerve Complications of Thyroid Surgery: (1) External Branch of Superior Laryngeal Nerve (EBSLN): Travels in close anatomical proximity to the SUPERIOR THYROID ARTERY at the superior pole of the thyroid gland. To avoid injuring EBSLN, the superior thyroid vessels MUST be ligated individually DOWN LOW directly on the thyroid capsule. Innervation: EBSLN is the SOLE motor nerve to the CRICOTHYROID MUSCLE (the intrinsic tensor of the vocal cords). Injury causes loss of vocal cord tension and elongation, resulting in inability to produce high-pitched sounds / high registers, vocal fatigue, and monotonic voice ('voice of Galli-Curci', devastating for professional singers and public speakers!). (2) Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (RLN): Runs in the Tracheoesophageal Groove in intimate association with the INFERIOR THYROID ARTERY and the Ligament of Berry. Inferior thyroid artery branches must be ligated individually PERIPHERALLY on the capsule. RLN innervates ALL intrinsic laryngeal muscles EXCEPT the cricothyroid. Unilateral RLN injury causes unilateral vocal cord paralysis (paramedian position), producing hoarseness of voice and ineffective cough. Bilateral RLN injury causes bilateral adduction of cords to the midline (Semon's law), causing acute catastrophic airway obstruction and stridor requiring IMMEDIATE EMERGENCY TRACHEOSTOMY! (3) Post-thyroidectomy Hypocalcemia: Accidental removal or devascularization of parathyroid glands leads to hypocalcemia (perioral numbness, Chvostek/Trousseau signs), peaking at 24-48 hours.",
