              "rationale": "Anaplastic Thyroid Carcinoma (ATC / Undifferentiated Carcinoma) is one of the most aggressive and lethal solid malignancies in humans, accounting for 1-2% of all thyroid cancers (predominantly in elderly females > 65-70 years, often arising from pre-existing long-standing goiter or dedifferentiation of papillary/follicular cancer). Hallmarks: Rapid, explosive growth (mass doubles within weeks), stone-hard woody fixation, direct invasion into adjacent neck structures: Trachea (stridor, hemoptysis), Esophagus (dysphagia), Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (hoarseness / vocal cord paralysis), and Cervical Sympathetic Chain (Horner's syndrome: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis). Molecular driver: Biallelic inactivation / mutation of TP53 tumor suppressor gene and TERT promoter mutations. Histopathology: Marked pleomorphism with spindle cells, bizarre multinucleated giant cells, squamoid cells, numerous atypical mitoses, and extensive coagulative necrosis. Prognosis: Abysmal; median survival is only 3 to 6 months from diagnosis (< 10-15% survive 1 year). By definition, ALL Anaplastic Thyroid Carcinomas are classified as STAGE IV at diagnosis (IVa: intrathyroidal, IVb: gross extrathyroidal extension, IVc: distant metastasis). Management is predominantly palliative: securing the airway (palliative tracheostomy or stent), palliative radiation and chemotherapy, or targeted therapy (Dabrafenib + Trametinib if BRAF V600E mutated).",
