              "rationale": "Chronic Venous Insufficiency (CVI) is caused by valvular incompetence in the superficial veins (Great/Small Saphenous), deep veins, or Perforating Veins (Cockett's, Boyd's, Dodd's perforators). Under normal conditions, the 'Calf Muscle Pump' (Peripheral Heart) empties deep veins and lowers venous pressure during walking. When valves are incompetent, calf muscle contraction forces blood retrogradely into superficial veins, producing AMBULATORY VENOUS HYPERTENSION. High venous pressure transmits to dermal microcirculation, causing extravasation of red blood cells into the dermis. Macrophage lysis of RBCs releases hemosiderin pigment, producing brown-black stasis pigmentation (Stasis Dermatitis). Extravasated fibrinogen forms pericapillary fibrin cuffs, causing tissue hypoxia and chronic woody subcutaneous fibrosis known as LIPODERMATOSCLEROSIS (inverted 'champagne bottle' leg). VENOUS STASIS ULCERS: Located characteristically in the 'GAITER ZONE' (the lower 1/3 of the leg, specifically the MEDIAL MALLEOLUS, overlying Cockett's perforators). Ulcer features: Shallow, irregular sloping borders, granulating base with heavy exudate, painless or aching (relieved by leg elevation!), with warm skin and NORMAL pedal pulses. CEAP Classification: C0 (no signs), C1 (telangiectasias/spider veins), C2 (varicose veins), C3 (edema), C4 (skin changes: C4a pigmentation/eczema, C4b lipodermatosclerosis), C5 (healed ulcer), C6 (active venous ulcer). Management: Compression Stockings (30-40 mmHg, mainstay of medical treatment!), leg elevation, and Endovenous Thermal Ablation (EVLA / RFA) or surgical ligation/stripping of incompetent saphenous veins.",
