              "rationale": "Subclavian Steal Syndrome is caused by severe atherosclerotic stenosis or occlusion of the Subclavian Artery PROXIMAL to the origin of the Vertebral Artery (most common on the LEFT side, 3:1 left-to-right ratio). Pathophysiology: When the ipsilateral arm is exercised, metabolic demand produces muscular vasodilation, dropping distal vascular resistance. Because blood cannot flow through the stenosed proximal subclavian artery, blood is sucked / diverted ('stolen') RETROGRADELY DOWN the ipsilateral Vertebral Artery from the basilar arterial system (Circle of Willis) into the distal subclavian artery to perfuse the working arm. This retrograde steal deprives the brainstem and cerebellum of perfusion, precipitating transient Vertebrobasilar Isufficiency: vertigo, ataxia, syncope, diplopia, and dysarthria provoked by arm exertion. Examination: Marked blood pressure differential (> 20-30 mmHg) between the two arms and delayed/diminished radial pulse. Treatment: Endovascular angioplasty with stenting of the proximal subclavian artery, or carotid-subclavian bypass.",
