            "exam_source": "NEET PG High-Yield Practice (NBE / AIIMS Pattern)",
            "topic": "Adrenal Incidentaloma - Surgical Criteria",
            "question": "An incidental 4.8 cm solitary left adrenal mass is discovered on CT in a 52-year-old male evaluated for kidney stones. Non-contrast CT demonstrates an attenuation of 24 Hounsfield Units (HU) and contrast washout is < 40% at 15 minutes. What features of an Adrenal Incidentaloma indicate surgical adrenalectomy?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Size >= 4 cm, Non-contrast attenuation > 10 HU, Delayed washout < 50%, or ANY biochemical functionality",
              "B": "Size > 1 cm only",
              "C": "Attenuation < 0 HU",
              "D": "Washout > 60%"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Adrenal Incidentaloma is an unsuspected adrenal mass (> 1 cm) detected on imaging performed for unrelated reasons. Evaluation requires two steps: (1) Biochemical Evaluation (mandatory for ALL adrenal incidentalomas to rule out hormonal hypersecretion): 1-mg overnight Dexamethasone suppression test (Cushing's), 24-hr urinary or plasma metanephrines (Pheochromocytoma), and Aldosterone-to-renin ratio if hypertensive (Conn's). (2) Radiological Assessment for Malignancy (Adrenocortical Carcinoma / ACC): Benign adenomas are lipid-rich (non-contrast CT attenuation <= 10 Hounsfield Units [HU]) and have rapid contrast washout (> 60% absolute washout at 15 mins). High malignancy risk features: (a) Size >= 4 cm, (b) Non-contrast attenuation > 10-20 HU (lipid-poor), (c) Delayed contrast washout < 50%, (d) Irregular margins and heterogeneous calcifications. Indications for Laparoscopic/Open Adrenalectomy: ANY functionally active tumor, OR any lesion with imaging features suspicious for malignancy (size >= 4 cm or HU > 10 with delayed washout).",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "CORRECT: Adrenal Incidentaloma is an unsuspected adrenal mass (> 1 cm) detected on imaging performed for unrelated reasons. Evaluation requires two steps: (1) Biochemical Evaluation (mandatory for ALL adrenal incidentalomas to rule out hormonal hypersecretion): 1-mg overnight Dexamethasone suppression test (Cushing's), 24-hr urinary or plasma metanephrines (Pheochromocytoma), and Aldosterone-to-renin ratio if hypertensive (Conn's). (2) Radiological Assessment for Malignancy (Adrenocortical Carcinoma / ACC): Benign adenomas are lipid-rich (non-contrast CT attenuation <= 10 Hounsfield Units [HU]) and have rapid contrast washout (> 60% absolute washout at 15 mins). High malignancy risk features: (a) Size >= 4 cm, (b) Non-contrast attenuation > 10-20 HU (lipid-poor), (c) Delayed contrast washout < 50%, (d) Irregular margins and heterogeneous calcifications. Indications for Laparoscopic/Open Adrenalectomy: ANY functionally active tumor, OR any lesion with imaging features suspicious for malignancy (size >= 4 cm or HU > 10 with delayed washout).",
                "B": "INCORRECT: Size > 1 cm only is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "C": "INCORRECT: Attenuation < 0 HU is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "D": "INCORRECT: Washout > 60% is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Adrenal Incidentaloma Rules: Step 1: Biochemical workup in ALL patients (Rule out Pheochromocytoma, Cushing, Aldosteronism). Step 2: CT criteria for Malignancy (Adrenocortical Carcinoma): Size >= 4 cm + Attenuation > 10 HU + Delayed washout < 50%. Surgical Adrenalectomy is indicated for ANY functioning mass OR size >= 4 cm."
            },
