            "topic": "Raynaud's Disease vs Raynaud's Phenomenon",
            "question": "A 22-year-old female presents with episodic color changes in her fingers on exposure to cold: initially the fingers turn stark white (pallor), then deep blue (cyanosis), and finally bright red and painful (rubor / hyperemia) upon rewarming. What microvascular examination distinguishes benign primary Raynaud's Disease from secondary Raynaud's Phenomenon associated with systemic sclerosis?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Nailfold Capillaroscopy (normal hairpin capillary loops in primary Raynaud's; giant dilated capillaries with capillary drop-out and avascular areas in secondary Raynaud's)",
              "B": "Serum uric acid",
              "C": "Blood culture",
              "D": "Plain hand radiograph"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Raynaud's Phenomenon is an episodic, reversible vasospasm of digital arteries and arterioles in response to cold exposure or emotional stress. Classical Triphasic Color Sequence: (1) White (Pallor - intense arteriolar vasospasm causing digital ischemia), (2) Blue (Cyanosis - deoxygenation of pooled static blood in capillaries), and (3) Red (Rubor - reactive hyperemic reperfusion with throbbing pain). Classification: (1) Primary Raynaud's (Raynaud's Disease, 80-90%): Symmetric, young females (15-30 yrs), NO underlying autoimmune disease, normal inflammatory markers, negative ANA, and NORMAL NAILFOLD CAPILLAROSCOPY (regular, uniform, hairpin capillary loops). Carries a benign course with zero ischemic tissue loss / ulceration. First-line therapy: Cold avoidance, smoking cessation, and Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (oral Amlodipine or Nifedipine). (2) Secondary Raynaud's (Raynaud's Phenomenon): Asymmetric, older onset, associated with systemic connective tissue diseases (Systemic Sclerosis / Scleroderma in > 90%, SLE, Mixed Connective Tissue Disease). Hallmarks: Positive ANA, digital pitting scars / gangrene, and ABNORMAL NAILFOLD CAPILLAROSCOPY: enlarged, giant tortuous capillary loops with microhemorrhages and extensive areas of capillary drop-out / avascularity (indicates structural microangiopathy).",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "CORRECT: Raynaud's Phenomenon is an episodic, reversible vasospasm of digital arteries and arterioles in response to cold exposure or emotional stress. Classical Triphasic Color Sequence: (1) White (Pallor - intense arteriolar vasospasm causing digital ischemia), (2) Blue (Cyanosis - deoxygenation of pooled static blood in capillaries), and (3) Red (Rubor - reactive hyperemic reperfusion with throbbing pain). Classification: (1) Primary Raynaud's (Raynaud's Disease, 80-90%): Symmetric, young females (15-30 yrs), NO underlying autoimmune disease, normal inflammatory markers, negative ANA, and NORMAL NAILFOLD CAPILLAROSCOPY (regular, uniform, hairpin capillary loops). Carries a benign course with zero ischemic tissue loss / ulceration. First-line therapy: Cold avoidance, smoking cessation, and Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (oral Amlodipine or Nifedipine). (2) Secondary Raynaud's (Raynaud's Phenomenon): Asymmetric, older onset, associated with systemic connective tissue diseases (Systemic Sclerosis / Scleroderma in > 90%, SLE, Mixed Connective Tissue Disease). Hallmarks: Positive ANA, digital pitting scars / gangrene, and ABNORMAL NAILFOLD CAPILLAROSCOPY: enlarged, giant tortuous capillary loops with microhemorrhages and extensive areas of capillary drop-out / avascularity (indicates structural microangiopathy).",
                "B": "INCORRECT: Serum uric acid is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "C": "INCORRECT: Blood culture is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "D": "INCORRECT: Plain hand radiograph is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Raynaud's Master Pearls: Triphasic Color Changes: White (Pallor/Ischemia) -> Blue (Cyanosis/Deoxygenation) -> Red (Rubor/Reperfusion). Primary Raynaud's: Benign, young women, no tissue necrosis, Normal Nailfold Capillaroscopy. Secondary Raynaud's: Connective tissue disease (#1 is Systemic Sclerosis!). Abnormal Nailfold Capillaroscopy (giant loops, capillary drop-out), digital ulcers / pitting scars. First-line medical therapy = Calcium Channel Blockers (Nifedipine / Amlodipine)."
            },
