            "id": "OBGY_PYQ_001",
            "question_type": "PYQ",
            "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024 Recall",
            "topic": "Preeclampsia & Magnesium Sulfate",
            "question": "A 24-year-old primigravida at 34 weeks gestation with severe preeclampsia is receiving a continuous intravenous Magnesium Sulfate infusion for seizure prophylaxis. On physical examination, her patellar deep tendon reflex is completely absent, respiratory rate is 10 breaths/min, and urine output is 15 mL/hr. What is the immediate life-saving antidote?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Intravenous 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 mL given slowly over 10 minutes)",
              "B": "Intravenous Naloxone 0.4 mg",
              "C": "Intravenous Flumazenil 0.5 mg",
              "D": "Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Loss of the patellar reflex is the earliest clinical sign of magnesium toxicity, occurring at serum levels of 8-10 mEq/L, followed by respiratory depression (< 12/min) at 12-15 mEq/L and cardiac arrest (> 15 mEq/L). The immediate management is to STOP the magnesium infusion and administer INTRAVENOUS 10% CALCIUM GLUCONATE (10 mL / 1 g IV over 10 mins) as a direct antagonist.",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "Correct. Loss of the patellar reflex is the earliest clinical sign of magnesium toxicity, occurring at serum levels of 8-10 mEq/L...",
                "B": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Intravenous Naloxone 0.4 mg).",
                "C": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Intravenous Flumazenil 0.5 mg).",
                "D": "Incorrect choice for this clinical presentation (Intravenous Sodium Bicarbonate)."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Magnesium Toxicity Antidote = IV 10% Calcium Gluconate (10 mL). Patellar reflex lost first (8-10 mEq/L) -> Respiratory arrest (12-15 mEq/L) -> Cardiac arrest (> 15 mEq/L). Always monitor urine output (Mg cleared by kidney!)."
            },
