              "rationale": "Preterm Labor (regular contractions with cervical change between 20 and 36+6 weeks): Antenatal Interventions to Improve Neonatal Outcomes: (1) ANTENATAL CORTICOSTEROIDS: Indicated between 24 and 34 weeks of gestation (and up to 36+6 weeks in selected cases) for impending preterm delivery. Steroids induce alveolar Type II pneumocyte maturation, stimulating SURFACTANT synthesis and lung compliance. Proven Benefits: Dramatically reduces Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS), Intraventricular Hemorrhage (IVH), Necrotizing Enterocolitis (NEC), and neonatal mortality! Regimen: BETAMETHASONE: 12 mg IM, 2 doses given 24 hours apart (preferred over dexamethasone due to decreased periventricular leukomalacia); or Dexamethasone: 6 mg IM, 4 doses given 12 hours apart. Optimal benefit occurs between 24 hours and 7 days after the first dose. (2) TOCOLYTIC THERAPY: Tocolytics suppress contractions to DELAY DELIVERY FOR 48 HOURS, providing the critical window needed to complete antenatal corticosteroids and transfer the mother to a tertiary neonatal ICU. First-Line Tocolytics: NIFEDIPINE (Oral Calcium Channel Blocker - inhibits myometrial calcium influx, superior maternal safety profile and efficacy) or ATOSIBAN (Oxytocin receptor antagonist). Indomethacin (COX inhibitor) is used if < 32 weeks (contraindicated after 32 weeks due to premature closure of ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios!). Terbutaline (beta-2 agonist) is limited due to maternal tachycardia/pulmonary edema. (3) NEUROPROTECTION: Intravenous MAGNESIUM SULFATE is administered for neuroprotection if delivery is imminent < 32 weeks (reduces Cerebral Palsy!).",
