              "rationale": "Preterm Premature Rupture of Membranes (PPROM) is membrane rupture prior to 37 weeks gestation before the onset of labor. Confirmatory Diagnostics: (1) Sterile Speculum Examination: Direct visualization of clear fluid pooling in the posterior vaginal fornix or leaking through cervical os on Valsalva/cough (digital vaginal exam is strictly avoided to prevent ascending infection!); (2) NITRAZINE TEST: Normal vaginal pH is acidic (3.8-4.5); Amniotic fluid is alkaline (pH 7.0-7.5). Amniotic fluid turns yellow Nitrazine paper deep BLUE (pH > 6.5; false positives: semen, blood, bacterial vaginosis); (3) FERNING TEST (Arborization): Amniotic fluid dried on a glass slide under light microscopy demonstrates pathognomonic delicate, palm-leaf-like dendritic 'FERNING PATTERN' (due to high sodium chloride and protein content); (4) AmniSure / Actim PROM (detects placental alpha-microglobulin-1 / PAMG-1 or IGFBP-1, > 98% sensitive). Management of PPROM < 34 weeks without infection: Expectant management (Antenatal corticosteroids + Latency Antibiotics [Ampicillin + Erythromycin / Azithromycin for 7-10 days to prolong latency and prevent neonatal sepsis]). CHORIOAMNIONITIS (Intra-amniotic Infection): Diagnostic Criteria: Maternal Fever (>= 38 C) PLUS >= 2 of: Maternal tachycardia (> 100), Fetal tachycardia (> 160), Uterine tenderness, Foul purulent discharge, or maternal leukocytosis (> 15,000). CRITICAL RULE: Chorioamnionitis is an ABSOLUTE INDICATION FOR IMMEDIATE DELIVERY (along with broad-spectrum IV Ampicillin + Gentamicin + Metronidazole) REGARDLESS OF GESTATIONAL AGE! Tocolysis and expectant management are strictly CONTRAINDICATED in chorioamnionitis.",
