              "rationale": "Asherman's Syndrome is the acquired destruction of the basal endometrial layer (stratum basalis) resulting in partial or complete obliteration of the uterine cavity by fibrous adhesions (Intrauterine Synechiae). Most common trigger: VIGOROUS CURETTAGE (D&C) performed on a soft, gravid, postpartum or post-abortal uterus (or genital tuberculosis in developing countries). Classical Sign: SECONDARY AMENORRHEA (or hypomenorrhea) with normal ovulatory cycles. Progesterone Challenge Test is NEGATIVE; Combined Estrogen-Progesterone Challenge Test is NEGATIVE (failure of withdrawal bleeding confirms an anatomical outflow tract defect!). Diagnostic gold standard: Hysteroscopy (or Hysterosalpingography / HSG showing irregular filling defects). Treatment of Choice: HYSTEROSCOPIC ADHESIOLYSIS (sharp cold-scissors dissection of adhesions) followed by high-dose oral Estrogen (to stimulate endometrial regeneration) and an intrauterine stent (Pediatric Foley balloon or Cook balloon) to prevent adhesion reformation.",
