              "rationale": "Vaginitis / Vaginal Discharge Master Differential Diagnosis Table: (1) BACTERIAL VAGINOSIS (BV): Polymicrobial dysbiosis: Loss of normal hydrogen-peroxide-producing Lactobacillus acidophilus and massive overgrowth of anaerobic bacteria (Gardnerella vaginalis, Atopobium vaginae, Mobiluncus). Amsel's Diagnostic Criteria (requires >= 3 of 4): (a) Thin, homogenous, greyish-white adherent discharge; (b) Vaginal pH > 4.5 (elevated, typically 5.0-6.0); (c) POSITIVE WHIFF / AMINE TEST: Addition of 1 drop of 10% Potassium Hydroxide (KOH) to vaginal fluid immediately liberates a strong, volatile, putrid 'FISHY' AMINE ODOR (due to volatilization of cadaverine and putrescine); (d) CLUE CELLS: Saline wet mount shows vaginal squamous epithelial cells whose borders are obscured, stippled, and studded by thousands of adherent coccobacilli (> 20% clue cells). Absence of PMN neutrophils (it is a vaginosis, not an -itis!). Treatment: Oral Metronidazole (500 mg bid for 7 days) or intravaginal Metronidazole / Clindamycin gel. (2) TRICHOMONIASIS: Sexually Transmitted Infection caused by Trichomonas vaginalis (flagellated parasitic protozoan). Clinical Presentation: Profuse, thin, FROTHY, bubbly, GREEN-YELLOW, malodorous purulent discharge, intense vulvar pruritus, dysuria. Physical Exam: 'STRAWBERRY CERVIX' (Colpitis Macularis: punctate mucosal microhemorrhages across cervix and upper vagina in 20%). Vaginal pH > 4.5-5.5 (elevated). Saline Wet Mount: Motile, pear-shaped FLAGELLATED TROPHOZOITES exhibiting jerky, corkscrew motility + abundant PMN leukocytes. Treatment: Oral Metronidazole (500 mg bid for 7 days, or 2g single dose). MANDATORY: SEXUAL PARTNER MUST BE TREATED SIMULTANEOUSLY! (3) VULVOVAGINAL CANDIDIASIS: Opportunistic fungal infection (Candida albicans in 85-90%). Risk Factors: Antibiotic use, diabetes mellitus, high estrogen (pregnancy, OCPs), immunosuppression. Presentation: SEVERE INTENSE VULVAR PRURITUS (itching is cardinal!), burning, dyspareunia, and THICK, WHITE, ODORLESS, 'CURD-LIKE' OR 'COTTAGE-CHEESE' DISCHARGE adhering to erythematous vaginal walls. Crucial Distinguishing Clue: VAGINAL pH IS STRICTLY NORMAL AND ACIDIC (< 4.5; typically 4.0-4.4). Whiff test is negative. 10% KOH Mount: Budding yeast blastospores and elongated PSEUDOHYPHAE. Treatment: Topical Azoles (Clotrimazole vaginal pessary) or single-dose Oral Fluconazole (150 mg).",
