              "rationale": "Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal disorder (1 in 700 live births). Cytogenetics: (1) Non-Disjunction (95%): Maternal meiotic non-disjunction (strongly linked to advanced maternal age > 35 years; recurrence risk ~1%); (2) Robertsonian Translocation (4%): Translocation t(14;21) or t(21;21); maternal carrier has 10-15% recurrence risk; (3) Mosaicism (1%). Cardinal Phenotypic Signs: (1) Marked Hypotonia ('floppy infant') and hyperflexible joints (most consistent neurological sign in neonates!); (2) Craniofacial: Brachycephaly with flat occiput, upward-slanting palpebral fissures, epicanthal folds, flat nasal bridge, Brushfield Spots (speckled ring of white/grey spots on the iris periphery), small low-set ears, macroglossia with protruding tongue; (3) Hands & Feet: SIMIAN CREASE (single transverse palmar crease in 50%), Clinodactyly (inward curving of 5th digit), and Sandal Gap (wide gap between 1st and 2nd toes). CONGENITAL HEART DEFECTS (present in 40-50%): ATRIOVENTRICULAR SEPTAL DEFECT (AVSD / Complete Endocardial Cushion Defect, 40-50% - most common defect!), followed by VSD and ASD. Gastrointestinal Anomalies: DUODENAL ATRESIA (associated with Down syndrome in 30%; presents with bilious vomiting on day 1 and 'Double-Bubble Sign' on X-ray!), Hirschsprung disease, and Celiac disease. Hematologic Malignancy: Acute Megakaryoblastic Leukemia (AML-M7) in children < 3 years, and ALL in children > 3 years (GATA1 mutations). Atlantoaxial instability (screen with cervical X-ray before sports!).",
