              "rationale": "Klinefelter Syndrome is the most common sex chromosome aneuploidy in males (1 in 600 live male births). Karyotype: 47,XXY (85-90%, due to meiotic non-disjunction of maternal or paternal X chromosomes; 1 Barr body seen on buccal smear) or mosaicism (46,XY/47,XXY). Pathophysiology: Seminiferous tubule dysgenesis and progressive hyalinization/fibrosis of tubules, destroying Sertoli cells (causing azoospermia and undetectable Inhibin B) and Leydig cell dysfunction (causing subnormal testosterone production). Cardinal Features: (1) Tall stature with EUNUCHOID PROPORTIONS (decreased upper-to-lower segment ratio, arm span > height by > 5 cm due to delayed epiphyseal fusion); (2) SMALL, HARD, ATROPHIC TESTES (volume < 4 mL or length < 2 cm; pathognomonic physical sign!); (3) GYNECOMASTIA (in 50-80%; carries a 20-50-fold increased risk of MALE BREAST CANCER!); (4) Sparse body/facial hair and female escutcheon; (5) Mild cognitive/language deficits, executive dysfunction. Endocrine Profile: HYPERGONADOTROPIC HYPOGONADISM (Markedly elevated FSH due to lack of inhibin B, elevated LH due to low negative feedback, and low/borderline testosterone). Semen: Complete azoospermia. Associated Malignancies: Extragonadal germ cell tumors (mediastinal non-seminomatous teratoma), male breast cancer, and non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Treatment: Lifelong Testosterone replacement therapy starting in adolescence.",
