            "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024",
            "topic": "Congenital Heart Disease - Tetralogy of Fallot",
            "question": "A 10-month-old infant is brought to the pediatric clinic with cyanosis that worsens during crying spells ('tet spells'). During these paroxysms of agitation, the child spontaneously squats to relieve his dyspnea. Chest radiograph demonstrates an uptilted cardiac apex with concavity in the pulmonary artery segment, resembling a wooden shoe or 'boot-shaped heart' (coeur en sabot). What physiological hemodynamic effect does squatting produce to terminate a hypercyanotic spell?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Increases systemic vascular resistance (SVR), reversing right-to-left shunting across the VSD",
              "B": "Decreases systemic venous return to relieve right ventricular strain",
              "C": "Selectively relaxes infundibular pulmonic stenosis",
              "D": "Decreases left ventricular afterload to promote systemic forward flow"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) is the most common cyanotic congenital heart disease beyond the neonatal period, consisting of: 1. Pulmonary infundibular stenosis (primary determinant of severity). 2. Right Ventricular Hypertrophy. 3. Overriding aorta. 4. Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD). During 'tet spells', acute spasm of the infundibular right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT) or a drop in systemic vascular resistance (SVR) surges deoxygenated venous blood across the VSD into the overriding aorta (right-to-left shunt), precipitating severe hypoxemic cyanosis. SQUATTING compresses femoral arteries and veins, markedly INCREASING SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR / LV afterload). Because LV pressure now exceeds RV pressure, the right-to-left shunt is reversed or diminished, driving more blood through the stenotic RVOT into pulmonary circulation for oxygenation.",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "Correct. Squatting increases SVR, reducing right-to-left shunting across the VSD.",
                "B": "Squatting actually increases venous return, but the primary therapeutic effect is increasing SVR.",
                "C": "Squatting does not relax the infundibulum; beta-blockers (propranolol) or morphine achieve infundibular relaxation.",
                "D": "Squatting increases, not decreases, left ventricular afterload."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF): Boot-shaped heart (coeur en sabot) on X-ray. Primary determinant of cyanosis = SEVERITY OF RVOT / PULMONIC STENOSIS. Hypercyanotic 'Tet Spell' Management: Knee-chest position / squatting (increases SVR) + 100% Oxygen + IV Morphine (sedation) + IV Fluids + IV Propranolol (relieves infundibular spasm) + IV Phenylephrine (increases SVR)."
            },
            "subject_id": "PEDIATRICS",
            "subject_name": "Pediatrics"
