              "rationale": "Hypoxic-Ischemic Encephalopathy (HIE): Perinatal asphyxia triggers a biphasic brain injury: Primary energy failure (immediate ATP depletion, membrane depolarization, cytotoxic edema) followed after a latent period of 6 hours by Secondary Energy Failure (mitochondrial failure, excitotoxicity, free radical release, caspase activation, and delayed neuronal apoptosis). Sarnat & Sarnat Staging of HIE: (1) Stage I (Mild): Hyperalert, irritable, tachycardia, dilated pupils, normal tone or hyperreflexia, Moro exaggerated, NO SEIZURES; (2) STAGE II (MODERATE): Lethargic, obtunded, hypotonic, constricted pupils, weak/absent Moro and suck reflexes, frequent MULTIFOCAL SEIZURES; (3) Stage III (Severe): Deep stupor or coma, flaccid, absent reflexes, non-reactive pupils, status epilepticus, flat-trace on amplitude-integrated EEG (aEEG). DEFINITIVE NEUROPROTECTIVE THERAPY: THERAPEUTIC HYPOTHERMIA (Targeted Temperature Management / Whole-Body Cooling or Selective Head Cooling). Inclusion Criteria: Gestational age >= 36 weeks, birth weight >= 1800g, evidence of perinatal asphyxia (pH <= 7.00, base deficit >= 16 mmol/L, or 10-minute Apgar <= 5), and clinical evidence of Moderate-to-Severe HIE (Sarnat Stage II or III). Protocol: Core body temperature (esophageal or rectal) is cooled to 33.5 C (range 33.0 C to 34.0 C) using a cooling blanket, MAINTAINED FOR EXACTLY 72 HOURS, followed by slow rewarming (at <= 0.5 C per hour). CRITICAL TIME WINDOW: MUST BE INITIATED WITHIN 6 HOURS OF BIRTH (during the latent phase before secondary energy failure sets in!). Therapeutic hypothermia significantly reduces death and major neurodevelopmental disability/cerebral palsy at 18-24 months.",
