              "rationale": "Retinopathy of Prematurity (ROP): A vasoproliferative vitreoretinal disorder affecting the immature retinal vasculature of preterm infants, and a leading cause of childhood blindness. Pathophysiology: Phase 1 (Hyperoxic Phase: birth to ~32 weeks PMA): Relative hyperoxia and loss of maternal VEGF causes cessation of normal retinal vessel growth and capillary obliteration. Phase 2 (Hypoxic-Vasoproliferative Phase: 32-34+ weeks PMA): As retinal metabolic demands grow, the non-vascularized peripheral retina becomes hypoxic, triggering massive up-regulation of VEGF and erythropoietin, producing abnormal neovascularization, fibrous ridge formation, tractional retinal detachment, and blindness (fibrolental fibroplasia). Screening Criteria (Indian Guidelines / NNF): (1) ALL INFANTS BORN AT < 34 WEEKS GESTATION, OR (2) BIRTH WEIGHT < 1,750 GRAMS, OR (3) Selected infants 34-36 weeks / 1,750-2,000g with risk factors (prolonged oxygen, sepsis, blood transfusions, RDS, cardiorespiratory instability). TIMING OF FIRST SCREENING: 'Tees Din Roshni Ke' (Within 30 Days of Birth!): At 4 WEEKS (28-30 days) of chronological age or 31-32 weeks Post-Menstrual Age (PMA), whichever is later. (If born < 28 weeks, screen at 2-3 weeks). Examination: Indirect Ophthalmoscopy with scleral indentation after pupillary dilation (Tropicamide 0.5% + Phenylephrine 2.5%). ICROP Staging: Stage 1 = Demarcation line; Stage 2 = Ridge; Stage 3 = Ridge with extraretinal fibrovascular proliferation; Stage 4 = Partial retinal detachment; Stage 5 = Total retinal detachment. 'Plus Disease' (dilatation and tortuosity of retinal arterioles in posterior pole) indicates severe active disease. Treatment: Laser Photocoagulation of avascular peripheral retina or Intravitreal Anti-VEGF (Bevacizumab / Ranibizumab).",
