            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Fever, petechial rash, neck stiffness, and CSF findings (high neutrophils, low glucose) indicate bacterial\nmeningitis. b) Outline the diagnostic tests and immediate management. (3 marks) Answer: Diagnostic Tests: 1) Lumbar puncture\nconfirms meningitis (high neutrophils, low glucose, high protein). 2) Blood culture identifies the organism (e.g., Neisseria\nmeningitidis). 3) CRP/ESR assesses inflammation. Management: Start IV ceftriaxone (100 mg/kg/day) and vancomycin (60\nmg/kg/day) empirically. Administer dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg) to reduce inflammation. Control seizures with IV lorazepam\n(0.1 mg/kg). Ensure airway and hydration. c) [Tough/Conceptual] Explain the mechanisms of blood-brain barrier disruption in\nbacterial meningitis and its implications for neurological sequelae. (5 marks) Answer: Bacterial meningitis disrupts the\nblood-brain barrier (BBB) via bacterial toxins (e.g., pneumococcal pneumolysin) and host cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β), increasing\nendothelial permeability. Neutrophil infiltration and matrix metalloproteinases degrade tight junctions, allowing bacterial entry into\nCSF . This causes cerebral edema, raised intracranial pressure, and neuronal injury. Neurological Sequelae: Persistent\ninflammation risks neuronal apoptosis, leading to hearing loss, seizures, or cognitive deficits. Early antibiotics and steroids\nmitigate BBB damage, but delayed treatment increases sequelae. Advanced therapies targeting cytokine pathways are under\ninvestigation. Diagram: Draw BBB with bacterial toxins, cytokines, and neutrophil infiltration. End of Detailed Solution Each\nanswer is comprehensive, providing correct options, explanations, and diagrams where required, tailored for MBBS students.\nTough, conceptual sub-questions test deeper understanding of pathophysiology and clinical implications.",
              "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in Pediatrics: Fever, petechial rash, neck stiffness, and CSF findings (high neutrophils, low glucose) indicate bacterial\nmeningitis."
            },
