              "rationale": "Wilms Tumor (Nephroblastoma) vs Neuroblastoma (Crucial Pediatric Oncology Distinction): (1) WILMS TUMOR (Nephroblastoma): The most common primary renal malignancy of childhood (peak age 2 to 4 years). Genetics: WT1 gene mutation on chromosome 11p13 (associated with WAGR Syndrome: Wilms, Aniridia, Genitourinary anomalies, Retardation; and Denys-Drash Syndrome: Wilms, pseudohermaphroditism, glomerulopathy) and WT2 gene on 11p15 (Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome: hemihyperplasia, macroglossia, omphalocele). Clinical Hallmarks: Asymptomatic, painless, SMOOTH, FIRM ABDOMINAL FLANK MASS (does NOT cross the midline!), microscopic hematuria, and hypertension (due to renin secretion). Triphasic Histology: Blastemal, epithelial, and stromal elements. Anaplasia (TP53 mutations) denotes unfavorable histology. Management: Radical nephrectomy with adjuvant chemotherapy (Vincristine + Dactinomycin); (2) NEUROBLASTOMA: Malignant tumor arising from neural crest cells in the adrenal medulla (50%) or sympathetic chain. Peak age < 2 years (most common extracranial solid tumor in infancy!). Clinical Hallmarks: Painful, irregular, nodular, HARD ABDOMINAL MASS THAT FREQUENTLY CROSSES THE MIDLINE! Encases and displaces aorta and renal vessels. Systemic symptoms: Opsoclonus-Myoclonus Syndrome ('Dancing eyes, dancing feet' paraneoplastic ataxia), periorbital ecchymoses ('Raccoon Eyes' due to orbital metastasis), intractable diarrhea (VIP secretion), and hypertension. Radiography: CT reveals FINE SPECKLED CALCIFICATIONS (present in 85-90% of neuroblastomas, rare in Wilms!). Urine Markers: ELEVATED URINARY CATECHOLAMINE METABOLITES (Homovanillic Acid / HVA and Vanillylmandelic Acid / VMA). Genetics: N-myc (MYCN) amplification signifies aggressive tumor and poor prognosis.",
