              "rationale": "Croup (Acute Laryngotracheobronchitis): The most common cause of acute upper airway obstruction in young children aged 6 MONTHS TO 3 YEARS (peak 1 to 2 years, predominantly in autumn/winter). Etiology: PARAINFLUENZA VIRUS (Type 1 is the most common cause, followed by Parainfluenza Type 2 and 3; other viruses include RSV, Adenovirus, Influenza). Pathophysiology: Viral infection causes diffuse inflammatory edema and mucosal hyperemia of the subglottic region (the narrowest portion of the pediatric airway, bounded by the complete cartilaginous cricoid ring). Cardinal Clinical Triad: (1) BARKING 'SEAL-LIKE' COUGH, (2) HOARSENESS OF VOICE, and (3) INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (accompanied by variable dyspnea and subcostal/sternal indrawing, characteristically worse at night). Classic Radiographic Hallmark: Soft-tissue AP radiograph of the neck demonstrates symmetrical tapering and narrowing of the subglottic trachea, known as the 'STEEPLE SIGN' or 'PENCIL-TIP SIGN' (cervical spine lateral view shows normal epiglottis, distinguishing it from epiglottitis!). Westley Croup Score assesses severity (stridor, retractions, air entry, cyanosis, consciousness). Management: (1) Mild Croup (barking cough, no stridor at rest): Reassurance, cool mist/humidification, and a SINGLE DOSE OF ORAL DEXAMETHASONE (0.15 to 0.6 mg/kg; long half-life of 36-72 hours, decreases subglottic edema within 1-2 hours); (2) Moderate-to-Severe Croup (stridor AT REST, retractions): NEBULIZED RACEMIC EPINEPHRINE (0.5 mL of 2.25% solution or 5 mL of 1:1,000 L-epinephrine: stimulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors causing rapid mucosal vasoconstriction and shrinkage of subglottic edema; works in 10-30 minutes!). Note: Observe for 2-4 hours after epinephrine due to risk of 'rebound' swelling when drug wears off.",
