            "exam_source": "Extreme Mode (Clinical Master Bank)",
            "topic": "Pediatrics Comprehensive",
            "question": "Case: A 2-year-old child presents with bow legs, frontal bossing, and delayed dentition. X-ray shows widened epiphyseal plates and fraying. Serum calcium is low, and 25- hydroxyvitamin D is deficient.",
            "options": {
              "A": "What is the most likely diagnosis? (2 marks)",
              "B": "Outline the diagnostic tests to confirm the condition and assess severity. (3 marks)",
              "C": "[Tough/Conceptual] Discuss the molecular mechanisms of vitamin D deficiency leading to bone deformities and their implications for treatment. (5 marks) Diagram: Vitamin D metabolism pathway. 195. Case: A 3-year-old presents with barking cough, stridor, and respiratory distress at night. Symptoms worsen with agitation, and oxygen saturation is 92%. (A) What is the most likely diagnosis? (2 marks) (B) Outline the management strategy, including pharmacological and supportive measures. (3 marks) (C) [Tough/Conceptual] Explain the immunological basis of croup and its implications for recurrent episodes. (5 marks) Diagram: Laryngeal inflammation in croup. 196. Case: A neonate at 36 weeks gestation is born limp, cyanotic, with a heart rate of 50 bpm and no respiratory effort. Apgar score at 1 minute is 2. (A) What is the most likely condition requiring intervention? (2 marks) (B) Describe the immediate steps of resuscitation as per NRP guidelines. (3 marks) (C) [Tough/Conceptual] Discuss the physiological principles underlying positive pressure ventilation in neonatal resuscitation and its impact on cerebral perfusion. (5 marks) Diagram: NRP resuscitation flowchart. 197. Case: A 4-year-old presents with fever, wheezing, and respiratory distress. Spirometry shows reversible airway obstruction. Symptoms are triggered by dust exposure, and there is a family history of atopy. (A) What is the most likely diagnosis? (2 marks) (B) Outline the diagnostic tests and long-term management plan. (3 marks) (C) [Tough/Conceptual] Discuss the role of Th2-mediated inflammation in asthma and its implications for novel therapies. (5 marks) Diagram: Th2 pathway in asthma. Confidential - For MBBS Examination & Revision Use Only 198. Case: A 6-year-old presents with fever, petechial rash, and neck stiffness for 2 days. Lumbar puncture shows high neutrophils, low glucose, and high protein. The child is irritable and has a seizure on admission. (A) What is the most likely diagnosis? (2 marks) (B) Outline the diagnostic tests and immediate management. (3 marks) (C) [Tough/Conceptual] Explain the mechanisms of blood-brain barrier disruption in bacterial meningitis and its implications for neurological sequelae. (5 marks) Diagram: Blood-brain barrier in meningitis. End of Question Paper Note: This question paper is designed to be challenging, with 200 marks covering 17 topics. It includes a mix of MCQs, SAQs, LAQs, and case-based questions with tough, conceptual sub- questions to test clinical and theoretical knowledge. Diagrams are mandatory where specified. 199. Cerebral malaria is most commonly caused by: (A) Plasmodium vivax (B) Plasmodium falciparum (C) Plasmodium malariae",
              "D": "Plasmodium ovale"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Bow legs, frontal bossing, delayed dentition, and X-ray findings with low vitamin D and calcium confirm rickets. b)\nOutline the diagnostic tests to confirm the condition and assess severity. (3 marks) Answer: Tests: 1) Serum 25-hydroxyvitamin\nD confirms deficiency (<20 ng/mL). 2) Serum calcium and phosphorus assess hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia. 3) Alkaline\nphosphatase is elevated due to bone turnover. 4) Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is elevated in secondary hyperparathyroidism. 5)\nX-ray shows widened epiphyseal plates and fraying. These tests confirm rickets and guide treatment intensity. c)\n[Tough/Conceptual] Discuss the molecular mechanisms of vitamin D deficiency leading to bone deformities and their implications\nfor treatment. (5 marks) Answer: Vitamin D deficiency impairs 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D synthesis, reducing calcium absorption in\nthe gut and kidneys. This triggers secondary hyperparathyroidism, increasing bone resorption and mobilizing calcium,\nweakening bones. At growth plates, unmineralized osteoid accumulates, causing widening and deformities (e.g., bow legs).\nChondrocyte maturation is disrupted, impairing endochondral ossification. Treatment: High-dose vitamin D (e.g., 2000- 5000\nIU/day) and calcium correct deficiencies, promoting mineralization. Monitoring PTH and alkaline phosphatase ensures response.\nLong-term prevention includes fortified foods and sunlight exposure. Diagram: Draw vitamin D activation (skin → liver → kidney)\nand calcium absorption.\n\nConfidential - For MBBS Examination & Revision Use Only",
              "high_yield_pearl": "Key finding in Pediatrics: Bow legs, frontal bossing, delayed dentition, and X-ray findings with low vitamin D and calcium confirm rickets."
            },
