              "rationale": "Pelvic Ring Fractures and Hemorrhagic Shock: High-energy blunt trauma disrupts the closed ring of the pelvis (sacrum, SI joints, ilium, pubic rami, pubic symphysis). YOUNG-BURGESS CLASSIFICATION: (1) APC (Anterior-Posterior Compression / 'Open-Book' Pelvis): Pubic symphysis diastasis (> 2.5 cm indicates disruption of anterior SI, sacrotuberous, and sacrospinous ligaments); (2) LC (Lateral Compression: most common overall, windswept pelvis); (3) VS (Vertical Shear: hemipelvis displacement, complete ligamentous disruption, severe instability). ETIOLOGY OF FATAL RETROPERITONEAL EXSANGUINATION: Over 85-90% of pelvic hemorrhage arises from LOW-PRESSURE, HIGH-VOLUME RETROPERITONEAL VENOUS PLEXUSES (Presacral venous plexus, Vesical/Paravesical plexus, and cancellous bone fracture surfaces); only 10-15% arises from arterial injury (Superior Gluteal, Internal Pudendal, Obturator, or Lateral Sacral branches of Internal Iliac Artery). When the pelvic ring 'springs open', the pelvic volume expands dramatically, eliminating the natural 'retroperitoneal tamponade effect'. IMMEDIATE BEDSIDE EMERGENCY INTERVENTION: RESUSCITATIVE PELVIC BINDING: A commercial pelvic binder (or folded bedsheet) is wrapped circumferentially to compress and 'close the book'. CORRECT ANATOMICAL PLACEMENT: MUST BE CENTERED PRECISELY OVER THE GREATER TROCHANTERS (placing over iliac crests is incorrect and ineffective!). Closing the pelvic volume restores retroperitoneal tamponade and halts venous oozing. If shock persists despite binding: Emergency Pelvic Angiography with Transcatheter Arterial Embolization (TAE) or Pre-peritoneal Pelvic Packing.",
