              "rationale": "Hip Dislocations: (1) POSTERIOR HIP DISLOCATION (85-90% - by far the most common type!): Mechanism: 'DASHBOARD INJURY' (impact applied to flexed knee and hip, driving femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabulum). CLASSICAL CLINICAL DEFORMITY: The injured limb is held in FLEXION, ADDUCTION, AND INTERNAL ROTATION with APPARENT SHORTENING (inflexible position; knee of affected side rests on normal thigh). Feared Complications: (a) SCIATIC NERVE INJURY (peroneal division predominantly injured in 10-20% -> loss of ankle dorsiflexion / Foot Drop and sensory loss over dorsum of foot); (b) AVASCULAR NECROSIS (AVN) OF THE FEMORAL HEAD: Caused by tearing of ascending retinacular vessels from the medial circumflex femoral artery. TIME-SENSITIVE EMERGENCY: Closed reduction must be performed WITHIN 6 HOURS of injury! After 6 hours, AVN risk increases exponentially; (2) ANTERIOR HIP DISLOCATION (10-15%): Mechanism: Forced extreme abduction, external rotation, and extension. CLASSICAL CLINICAL DEFORMITY: The injured limb is held in FLEXION (or extension in pubic type), ABDUCTION, AND EXTERNAL ROTATION with limb lengthening or normal length (Mnemonic: Anterior = Abduction + External rotation; Posterior = Adduction + Internal rotation!). Complications: Femoral nerve and femoral artery compression/injury; (3) Central Dislocation: Femoral head driven medial through comminuted acetabular floor into pelvis.",
