              "rationale": "Calcaneal Fractures: The MOST COMMONLY FRACTURED TARSAL BONE (accounts for 60% of all tarsal fractures). Mechanism: High-energy axial compression load (fall from height landing on heels, colloquially known as 'Don Juan' or 'Lover's Fracture'). Essex-Lopresti Classification: Tongue-Type vs Joint-Depression Type. Sanders CT Classification (based on posterior facet articular fracture lines): Types I through IV. RADIOGRAPHIC ANGLES ON LATERAL FOOT X-RAY: (1) BOHLER'S TUBER-JOINT ANGLE: Drawn between lines connecting the superior posterior tuberosity, subtalar articular facet, and anterior process of calcaneus. NORMAL BOHLER'S ANGLE = 20 TO 40 DEGREES. Intra-articular calcaneal compression fractures crush the posterior facet, severely REDUCING BOHLER'S ANGLE (< 20 DEGREES) or making it negative! (Restoration of Bohler's angle > 20 deg is a primary surgical goal); (2) Angle of Gissane (Crucial Angle): Normal is 120-145 degrees (widened in fractures). MANDATORY SYSTEMIC SCREENING: Because the axial force transmits directly up the skeleton, 10-15% OF PATIENTS WITH CALCANEUS FRACTURES HAVE ASSOCIATED THORACOLUMBAR COMPRESSION / BURST SPINAL FRACTURES (T12-L2) and 10% have bilateral calcaneal fractures! Full thoracic and lumbar spine radiographs are mandatory.",
