              "rationale": "Acute Compartment Syndrome (ACS): An orthopedic surgical emergency characterized by elevated tissue pressure within an inelastic, closed osteofascial compartment, leading to microvascular capillary hypoperfusion, tissue hypoxia, and irreversible muscle necrosis. Common Sites: Tibia (#1 most common: anterior compartment), forearm (volar compartment), thigh, foot, hand. Classical '6 Ps': Pain, Pressure, Paresthesia, Pallor, Paralysis, Pulselessness: (1) EARLIEST AND MOST SENSITIVE CLINICAL SIGN: SEVERE PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION TO INJURY, CHARACTERISTICALLY EXACERBATED BY PASSIVE STRETCHING OF THE INVOLVED COMPARTMENT MUSCLES (e.g. passive toe flexion stretches the anterior compartment muscles); (2) Paresthesias (early sign of nerve ischemia); (3) Tense, 'woody', hard swollen compartment on palpation; (4) VERY LATE SIGNS OF IRREVERSIBLE MYONECROSIS: Pulselessness, Pallor, and Paralysis (if pulses are absent, irreversible neuromuscular necrosis has already occurred!). OBJECTIVE DIAGNOSTIC MEASUREMENT: Stryker Needle Manometer: (a) Absolute Compartment Pressure >= 30 mmHg, OR (b) DELTA PRESSURE (Diastolic Blood Pressure minus Compartment Pressure) <= 30 mmHg (Delta P <= 30 is the gold standard diagnostic threshold!). SURGICAL EMERGENCY TREATMENT: EMERGENCY DOUBLE-INCISION FOUR-COMPARTMENT FASCIOTOMY OF THE LEG (Anterolateral incision decompressing anterior and lateral compartments; Posteromedial incision decompressing superficial and deep posterior compartments). Do NOT close skin primarily; delayed closure or split-thickness skin graft.",
