              "rationale": "Gustilo-Anderson Classification of Open Fractures: (1) GRADE I: Clean open wound < 1 cm in length; minimal soft tissue injury or contamination; low-energy simple transverse or oblique fracture (Infection risk 0-2%); (2) GRADE II: Open wound 1 to 10 cm in length; moderate soft tissue damage; moderate contamination; moderate bone comminution (Infection risk 2-7%); (3) GRADE III: High-energy open fracture with wound > 10 cm OR ANY open fracture with severe crushing, high contamination (soil/farmyard), high-velocity gunshot, or segmental fracture: (a) GRADE IIIA: Extensive soft tissue laceration/damage, but ADEQUATE LOCAL SOFT TISSUE IS AVAILABLE TO PROVIDE COMPLETE BONE COVERAGE without local or free flaps (Infection risk 10-15%); (b) GRADE IIIB: Extensive soft tissue loss with PERIOSTEAL STRIPPING AND BONE EXPOSURE; requires local rotational muscle flap or free microvascular tissue transfer for coverage (Infection risk 20-30%); (c) GRADE IIIC: Open fracture associated with an ARTERIAL INJURY REQUIRING IMMEDIATE VASCULAR REPAIR for limb viability, regardless of wound size (Infection risk 30-50%, high amputation rate!). ANTIBIOTIC MANAGEMENT PROTOCOL: Started immediately in the ED: Grade I & II = 1st-generation Cephalosporin (IV Cefazolin); Grade III = IV Cefazolin + AMINOGLYCOSIDE (IV Gentamicin for Gram-negative coverage); If farmyard/soil contamination = Add IV PENICILLIN G (or Metronidazole) for Clostridium perfringens (gas gangrene) coverage. Tetanus prophylaxis + Urgent operative debridement within 12-24 hours.",
