              "rationale": "Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES): A systemic life-threatening complication occurring in 1-3% of patients with isolated long-bone fractures (femur, tibia) or pelvic fractures. Pathophysiology: Mechanical intravasation of marrow fat droplets into ruptured venous sinusoids -> lodge in pulmonary microvasculature. Lipase breaks fat into toxic Free Fatty Acids (FFA), producing severe chemical pneumonitis, endothelial damage, acute ARDS, and systemic capillary leakage. Latent Period: Typically manifests 24 TO 72 HOURS post-trauma (rarely before 12 hours). GURD AND WILSON'S DIAGNOSTIC CRITERIA (Requires >= 1 Major + >= 4 Minor criteria): MAJOR CRITERIA (The Classic Triad): (1) PETECHIAL RASH: Pathognomonic physical sign! Non-palpable petechiae in a 'vest' distribution across the anterior neck, axillae, chest wall, and subconjunctiva (due to fat droplets occluding dermal capillaries, dissolves in 24-48 hrs; present in 50-60%); (2) RESPIRATORY INSUFFICIENCY / HYPOXEMIA: Dyspnea, tachypnea, PaO2 < 60 mmHg (FiO2 0.4); (3) NEUROLOGICAL CENTRAL DYSFUNCTION: Confusion, agitation, stupor, or coma disproportionate to hypoxemia and unrelated to intracranial trauma. Minor Criteria: Tachycardia (> 120 bpm), Fever (> 38 C), Retinal fat / petechiae on fundoscopy (Purtscher retinopathy), sudden drop in Hemoglobin (> 20%), Thrombocytopenia (< 150,000), Fat macroglobulinemia in urine or sputum. Radiograph: 'SNOWSTORM' / diffuse bilateral patchy pulmonary infiltrates. Prevention: EARLY DEFINITIVE OPERATIVE STABILIZATION of long-bone fractures within 24 hours. Treatment: High-flow oxygen, CPAP, lung-protective mechanical ventilation; steroids are controversial.",
