              "rationale": "Ulnar Nerve Neuropathy & The 'Ulnar Paradox': Sites of Ulnar Nerve Compression/Injury: (1) Proximal: At the Cubital Tunnel / behind Medial Epicondyle of humerus; (2) Distal: At Guyon's Canal (pisohamate hiatus at the wrist). Innervation: Forearm = Flexor Carpi Ulnaris (FCU) and medial half of Flexor Digitorum Profundus (FDP to digits 4 and 5); Hand = Hypothenar muscles, all Interossei (PAD and DAB), medial 2 Lumbricals (digits 4 and 5), and ADDUCTOR POLLICIS. Ulnar Claw Hand (Main en Griffe): Loss of lumbricals and interossei produces HYPEREXTENSION at the Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints (due to unopposed extensor digitorum) and HYPERFLEXION at the Interphalangeal (IP) joints (due to flexor digitorum profundus and superficialis). THE 'ULNAR PARADOX': A distal lesion at the wrist produces a MUCH MORE SEVERE AND GROTESQUE CLAW HAND than a proximal lesion at the elbow! Why? In a distal lesion (at Guyon's canal), the ulnar half of the FDP in the forearm is COMPLETELY INTACT; the working FDP actively pulls the denervated 4th and 5th digits into extreme flexion, worsening the claw deformity. In a high/proximal lesion (at the cubital tunnel), the FDP to digits 4 and 5 is paralyzed; without active finger flexion, the fingers hang relatively straight and clawing is much milder! (The closer the lesion is to the hand, the worse the deformity looks!). FROMENT'S SIGN: Evaluates Adductor Pollicis (ulnar nerve). Patient grips paper between thumb and index finger; paralyzed adductor pollicis forces compensatory flexion of the thumb IP joint via the MEDIAN-INNERVATED Flexor Pollicis Longus (FPL).",
