              "rationale": "Osteochondroma (Osteocartilaginous Exostosis): The MOST COMMON BENIGN BONE TUMOR (accounting for 35-40% of all benign bone neoplasms and 10% of all bone tumors). Pathogenesis: Aberrant displacement of lateral epiphyseal growth plate cartilage that grows through appositional endochondral ossification. Associated with loss-of-function mutations in EXT1 or EXT2 genes (Hereditary Multiple Exostoses / HME: autosomal dominant, multiple lesions, high risk of forearm deformity). Anatomical Location: METAPHYSIS of long bones (distal femur, proximal tibia, proximal humerus). Radiographic Pathognomonic Hallmarks: (1) BONY PROJECTION ARISING FROM THE METAPHYSIS POINTING AWAY FROM THE NEAREST ADJACENT JOINT (along the vector of muscular pull), and (2) ABSOLUTE DIAGNOSTIC PATHOGNOMONIC CRITERION: CONTINUITY OF BOTH THE CORTEX AND THE MEDULLARY CAVITY OF THE LESION WITH THE UNDERLYING NATIVE BONE (the marrow cavity of the stalk communicates directly with the marrow of the host bone!). Types: Pedunculated (stalk) or Sessile (broad-based). Growth stops when the patient reaches skeletal maturity (growth plate closure). WARNING SIGNS OF MALIGNANT TRANSFORMATION TO CHONDROSARCOMA (occurs in < 1% of solitary, 3-5% of multiple exostoses): (a) New pain or sudden enlargement of the lesion in a SKELETALLY MATURE ADULT, (b) THICKENING OF THE CARTILAGINOUS CAP > 1.5 TO 2.0 CM ON MRI (normal benign cap in adults is < 5-10 mm; a cartilaginous cap > 1.5-2 cm is a strong indicator of low-grade secondary Chondrosarcoma!), and (c) Irregular focal areas of osteolysis. Treatment: Simple surgical excision with complete removal of the cartilaginous cap and perichondrium.",
