              "rationale": "Osteoid Osteoma: A benign osteoblastic neoplasm accounting for 10% of all benign bone tumors (peak age 10 to 25 years; male:female 2-3:1). Anatomical Location: Diaphysis or metaphysis of long bones (proximal femur > tibia > posterior elements of spine). Pathophysiology: The lesion consists of a hypervascular vascular central NIDUS (< 1.5 to 2.0 cm in diameter) of interlacing osteoid trabeculae lined by osteoblasts, enclosed within dense, marked reactive host periosteal and endosteal cortical sclerosis. The neoplastic cells in the nidus synthesize ENORMOUS CONCENTRATIONS OF PROSTAGLANDINS (PGE2 and PGI2, up to 100-500 times normal bone levels!), which stimulate sensory unmyelinated nerve fibers within the nidus. CLASSICAL CLINICAL HALLMARK: SEVERE, BORING, LOCALIZED BONE PAIN THAT IS CHARACTERISTICALLY WORSE AT NIGHT (awakes patient from sleep) AND IS DRAMATICALLY, RAPIDLY RELIEVED BY ASPIRIN OR OTHER NSAIDs (due to COX-mediated inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis; 90% diagnostic accuracy!). Diagnostic Modality of Choice: HIGH-RESOLUTION THIN-SLICE CT SCAN: Identifies the round/oval radiolucent NIDUS (< 1.5 cm) containing a central punctate calcification ('target sign') surrounded by dense cortical sclerosis (Plain X-ray can obscure small intracortical nidus). Osteoid Osteoma vs Osteoblastoma: Osteoblastoma is histologically identical but LARGER (> 2.0 cm), commonly affects the spine/vertebral arch, does NOT respond predictably to aspirin, and causes progressive bone expansion. Treatment of Choice: PERCUTANEOUS CT-GUIDED RADIOFREQUENCY ABLATION (RFA: thermal coagulation of nidus at 90 C for 4-6 minutes; outpatient procedure, > 95% cure rate!).",
