              "rationale": "Septic Arthritis of the Hip (Pediatric Orthopedic Emergency): Acute bacterial infection of the joint space causing rapid proteolytic chondrolysis and permanent hip destruction within 24-48 hours! Causative Pathogens: S. aureus (#1 overall), Group B Strep (neonates), Neisseria gonorrhoeae (sexually active adolescents). Affected Hip Posture: Child holds hip in FLEXION, ABDUCTION, AND EXTERNAL ROTATION (the position that maximizes joint capsule volume and minimizes intracapsular pressure and pain). KOCHER CRITERIA (Predicts Septic Arthritis vs Benign Transient Synovitis): (1) INABILITY TO BEAR WEIGHT (non-weight bearing on affected side), (2) ORAL TEMPERATURE > 38.5 C (101.3 F), (3) ESR >= 40 mm/hr, (4) SERUM WBC COUNT > 12,000 /uL (C-Reactive Protein > 20 mg/L added as 5th criterion, most sensitive!). Probability of Septic Arthritis: 1 criterion = 3%; 2 criteria = 40%; 3 criteria = 93%; 4 criteria = 99%! DIAGNOSTIC GOLD STANDARD: ULTRASOUND-GUIDED ARTHROCENTESIS (HIP ASPIRATION): Diagnostic synovial fluid parameters for septic arthritis: (a) Turbid, purulent fluid, (b) SYNOVIAL WBC COUNT > 50,000 /uL (often > 100,000), (c) > 75-90% POLYMORPHONUCLEAR (PMN) NEUTROPHILS, (d) Synovial Glucose < 50 mg/dL (< 50% of blood glucose), (e) Gram stain and culture positive. EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT: IMMEDIATE SURGICAL ARTHROTOMY AND DRAINAGE (or arthroscopic debridement) + Intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics (Cefazolin or Vancomycin + Ceftriaxone) to prevent avascular necrosis of the femoral head and chondrolysis.",
