              "rationale": "Osteomalacia vs Rickets: Defective mineralization of newly synthesized organic bone osteoid matrix. Distinction: RICKETS occurs in growing CHILDREN BEFORE epiphyseal plate closure, characterized by defective mineralization of cartilage at epiphyseal growth plates; OSTEOMALACIA occurs in ADULTS AFTER epiphyseal growth plate closure, characterized by generalized accumulation of unmineralized osteoid along trabecular and cortical bone surfaces. Etiology: Vitamin D deficiency (malabsorption, celiac disease, gastrectomy, chronic liver or renal disease), hypophosphatemia, or Fanconi syndrome. Clinical Manifestations: Insidious, generalized, diffuse dull bone pain, exquisite bone tenderness (over sternum, anterior tibia), and PROXIMAL MUSCLE WEAKNESS / MYOPATHY (inability to rise from a chair or climb stairs without using arms, producing a characteristic 'WADDLING GAIT'). PATHOGNOMONIC RADIOGRAPHIC HALLMARK: LOOSER'S ZONES (MILKMAN'S PSEUDOFRACTURES / UMBAUZONEN): Bilateral, symmetrical, transverse, narrow radiolucent bands oriented perpendicular to the cortical bone surface with sclerotic margins, occurring classically on the COMPRESSION (CONCAVE) SIDES of bones: Medial femoral neck, pubic rami, inner border of scapula, ribs, and proximal ulna (represent cortical stress microfractures repaired with unmineralized osteoid!). Biochemical Profile: LOW OR NORMAL CALCIUM + LOW PHOSPHATE + ELEVATED ALKALINE PHOSPHATASE (ALP) + Secondary Elevated PTH + Low 25-(OH)-Vitamin D (< 10-15 ng/mL). Treatment: High-dose Cholecalciferol (Vitamin D3: 50,000 IU weekly for 8 weeks) + elemental Calcium.",
