              "rationale": "Chondrosarcoma: A malignant bone tumor characterized by the direct production of NEOPLASTIC CARTILAGINous MATRIX (hyaline or myxoid) without osteoid formation. Epidemiology: The THIRD most common primary malignant bone tumor overall (after Multiple Myeloma and Osteosarcoma), but the SECOND most common non-hematopoietic bone sarcoma. Age: SKELETALLY MATURE OLDER ADULTS (peak age 40 to 70 years; rare in children < 20!). Anatomical Location: Axial skeleton and proximal long bones: PELVIS (ilium #1), Proximal Femur, Proximal Humerus, Ribs, and Scapula. Radiographic Hallmarks: (1) Lytic expansile bone destruction, (2) Endosteal scalloping (> 2/3 cortical thickness is a key sign of malignancy!), (3) PATHOGNOMONIC 'RINGS AND ARCS' / 'POPCORN' / 'STIPPLED' CHONDROID CALCIFICATIONS (calcification of the cartilaginous matrix forming curvi-linear rings, arcs, and calcified pearls). CRITICAL ONCOLOGICAL VULNERABILITY & MANAGEMENT: Chondrosarcomas are INHERENTLY RESISTANT TO CONVENTIONAL SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY AND RADIATION THERAPY! Reasons: Abundant avascular, bradytrophic extracellular cartilage matrix prevents drug penetration, slow mitotic rate (low growth fraction), and high expression of P-glycoprotein multidrug resistance pump (MDR-1) and anti-apoptotic proteins (Bcl-2). Therefore, THE EXCLUSIVE STANDARD OF CARE AND ONLY CURATIVE TREATMENT IS EN BLOC WIDE SURGICAL EXCISION with histologically negative margins (intralesional curettage has an unacceptably high local recurrence rate!).",
