            "question": "A 13-year-old overweight boy (BMI 31 kg/m2) presents with a 6-week history of dull, aching right knee and medial thigh pain and an antalgic limp. Examination of the hip reveals obligatory external rotation during passive flexion and limited internal rotation. Anteroposterior pelvic radiograph demonstrates that a tangent line drawn along the superior margin of the femoral neck (Klein's Line) fails to intersect the lateral aspect of the femoral epiphysis (Trethowan's Sign). What is the definitive management of choice?",
