              "rationale": "Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE / Coxa Vara Adolescentium): An endocrine-mechanical disorder where the proximal femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and medially relative to the femoral neck through the hypertrophic zone of the physis. Demographics: Overweight / obese adolescent boys (age 11-15 years, during pubertal growth spurt; associated with hypothyroidism and growth hormone therapy). Clinical Presentation: Referred pain to the ipsilateral medial thigh and KNEE (via obturator nerve), antalgic limp, and PATHOGNOMONIC EXAM SIGN: OBLIGATORY EXTERNAL ROTATION OF THE HIP WHEN THE HIP IS FLEXED TO 90 DEGREES (loss of internal rotation). Radiographic Hallmarks: AP and frog-leg lateral pelvis X-rays: (1) KLEIN'S LINE fails to intersect the epiphysis (Trethowan's sign), (2) 'Ice-cream scoop slipping off cone' appearance, (3) Blanch of Steel sign (crescent of increased density on femoral neck due to overlapping slipped head). DEFINITIVE TREATMENT: URGENT IN-SITU SINGLE PERCUTANEOUS CANNULATED SCREW FIXATION across the physis to stabilize the epiphysis and prevent further slippage. CRITICAL RULE: FORCEFUL CLOSED REDUCTION IS STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED! Forceful manipulation tears posterior retinacular vessels, precipitating AVASCULAR NECROSIS (AVN) of the femoral head!",
