              "rationale": "Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis: (1) SPONDYLOLYSIS: A bony defect or stress fracture of the PARS INTERARTICULARIS (the isthmus of bone connecting the superior and inferior articular facets of a vertebra). Etiology: Repetitive hyperextension stress in young athletes (gymnasts, divers, cricket fast bowlers, football linemen). Site: Over 90% occur at L5 (followed by L4). Pathognomonic Radiograph: 45-DEGREE OBLIQUE LUMBAR SPINE X-RAY: Demonstrates the 'LACHAPELE SCOTTIE DOG' configuration: Snout = Transverse process; Eye = Pedicle; Ear = Superior articular facet; Foreleg = Inferior articular facet; Neck = PARS INTERARTICULARIS. A fracture of the pars appears as a radiolucent band across the dog's neck: 'SCOTTIE DOG WITH A COLLAR' (or decapitated dog); (2) SPONDYLOLISTHESIS: Forward / anterior subluxation or slippage of one vertebral body relative to the subjacent vertebra beneath it (most commonly L5 slipping anteriorly over S1). Types (Wiltse Classification): Type I (Dysplastic / congenital), Type II (Isthmic: due to bilateral pars fractures), Type III (Degenerative: intact pars, facet arthrosis in elderly, L4-L5 #1), Type IV (Traumatic), Type V (Pathologic). MEYERDING SLIP GRADING (Percentage of anterior displacement of superior vertebra on inferior vertebra): Grade I = 0 to 25% slippage; GRADE II = 25 TO 50% SLIPPAGE; Grade III = 50 to 75% slippage; Grade IV = 75 to 100% slippage; Grade V (Spondyloptosis) = > 100% slippage (complete ptosis of L5 anterior to S1, 'Inverted Napoleon Hat' / inverted bowler hat sign on AP pelvis). Management: Grade I-II = Activity modification, hamstring stretching, core strengthening, bracing; Grade III-V or progressive neurological deficit = Decompression and posterior instrumented lumbosacral fusion.",
