              "rationale": "Osteoarthritis (OA / Degenerative Joint Disease): The most common joint disorder worldwide. Pathophysiology: Progressive mechanical degradation and enzymatic breakdown of articular hyaline cartilage driven by chondrocyte matrix metalloproteinases (MMP-13, aggrecanases), accompanied by secondary subchondral bone remodeling, sclerosis, and low-grade synovial inflammation. Risk Factors: Advanced age, female sex, obesity (BMI > 30 increases knee OA risk 4-fold), previous joint trauma / meniscectomy, and repetitive occupational mechanical stress. Cardinal Clinical Features: (1) Pain is characteristically MECHANICAL: Worsens with joint activity and weight-bearing, RELIEVED BY REST; (2) MORNING STIFFNESS IS SHORT-LIVED (< 30 MINUTES; 'gel phenomenon' resolving within minutes of joint movement; contrast with RA where stiffness lasts > 1 hour!); (3) Coarse crepitus on passive range of motion; (4) Joint enlargement and bony tenderness. CLASSICAL RADIOGRAPHIC TETRAD (Weight-Bearing Radiographs): (a) ASYMMETRICAL JOINT SPACE NARROWING (medial compartment of knee > lateral; reflects focal cartilage loss), (b) SUBCHONDRAL SCLEROSIS / EBURNATION (dense, white bone beneath denuded cartilage due to increased compressive stress), (c) SUBCHONDRAL CYSTS / GEODES (synovial fluid forced under pressure through microfractures into cancellous marrow), and (d) MARGINAL OSTEOPHYTES (spurs of new fibrocartilaginous and bony proliferation at joint margins). HAND OSTEOARTHRITIS EPONYMS: (1) HEBERDEN'S NODES: Hard, bony palpable osteophytic swellings at the DISTAL INTERPHALANGEAL (DIP) JOINTS (DIP = Heberden; Mnemonic: 'D' comes after 'C', H is near D in alphabet); (2) BOUCHARD'S NODES: Bony osteophytic swellings at the PROXIMAL INTERPHALANGEAL (PIP) JOINTS (PIP = Bouchard). First Carpometacarpal (CMC) joint of thumb is also frequently involved ('squaring of hand').",
