            "question": "A 60-year-old diabetic male with severe peripheral sensory neuropathy presents with a swollen, warm, deformed foot with a collapsed medial longitudinal arch producing a 'Rocker-Bottom Foot'. Radiographs reveal massive joint destruction, bone debris, and dislocation of the tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) joints out of all proportion to the patient's mild pain. What is the diagnosis, what are the classic '6 Ds' of Charcot joint, and what condition historically caused Charcot joint of the knee?",
