            "question": "A 62-year-old male with a history of chronic alcohol consumption and diabetes presents with progressive thickening of the palm of his right hand. Examination reveals firm, painless, nodular puckering of the skin and thick longitudinal fibrous cords along the palmar aponeurosis, causing fixed flexion deformities of the Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and Proximal Interphalangeal (PIP) joints of the RING AND LITTLE FINGERS. The patient cannot lay his hand flat on the tabletop (Hueston's Tabletop Test positive). What tissue is pathologically affected, and what myofibroblastic proliferation drives it?",
