              "rationale": "Cervical Spondylosis: Degenerative osteoarthritis of the cervical spine producing: (1) Cervical Spondylotic Radiculopathy (nerve root compression): Unilateral dermatomal arm pain, paresthesias, and motor weakness. Provocative Test: SPURLING'S TEST (neck extension and lateral rotation toward affected side with downward axial compression reproduces radicular pain; highly specific > 90%); (2) CERVICAL SPONDYLOTIC MYELOPATHY (CSM): The MOST COMMON CAUSE OF SPINAL CORD DYSFUNCTION IN ADULTS > 55 YEARS! Direct compression of the spinal cord by osteophytic bars, hypertrophied ligamentum flavum, and herniated discs: Hallmarks: (a) Clumsiness and loss of fine motor dexterity of hands ('clumsy numb hands': difficulty buttoning clothes, dropping coins); (b) Broad-based, spastic, unsteady 'drunk-like' gait with stiffness; (c) UPPER MOTOR NEURON (UMN) SIGNS: Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes below lesion, positive Babinski sign, sustained ankle clonus, and HOFFMAN'S SIGN (flicking the distal phalanx of the middle finger triggers involuntary flexion and adduction of the thumb and index finger); (d) INVERTED SUPINATOR REFLEX (brachioradialis jerk tap produces finger flexion with diminished elbow flexion); (e) LHERMITTE'S SIGN (sudden electric shock radiating down spine into limbs on neck flexion). Investigation of Choice: MRI cervical spine (cord compression, myelomalacia hyperintensity on T2). Definitive Treatment: Surgical decompression (Anterior Cervical Discectomy and Fusion [ACDF] or Posterior Laminoplasty).",
