              "rationale": "Epicondylopathies of the Elbow: (1) LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS ('TENNIS ELBOW'): An overuse tendinopathy characterized by angiofibroblastic tendinosis (collagen degeneration, microtearing, hypervascularity, and lack of inflammatory cells) at the common extensor tendon origin. The ANTEROMEDIAL ORIGIN OF THE EXTENSOR CARPI RADIALIS BREVIS (ECRB) TENDON IS RESPONSIBLE IN > 90% OF CASES! (Occasionally involves extensor digitorum communis). Demographics: Peak age 35 to 55 years; repetitive wrist extension and forearm pronation/supination (tennis backhand, typing, plumbing). Cardinal Physical Tests: (a) Exquisite tenderness localized 1-2 cm distal and anterior to the lateral epicondyle; (b) COZEN'S TEST: Patient resists forced wrist flexion while maintaining wrist extension and radial deviation with elbow extended -> sharp lateral epicondylar pain!; (c) MILL'S TEST: Passive wrist flexion and pronation with elbow extended reproduces pain; (d) Maudsley's Test (pain on resisted middle finger extension); (2) MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS ('GOLFER'S ELBOW' / 'PITCHER'S ELBOW'): Degeneration of the common flexor-pronator tendon origin at the medial epicondyle: Principally PRONATOR TERES and FLEXOR CARPI RADIALIS (FCR) tendons. Provocative Test: Pain aggravated by RESISTED WRIST FLEXION and resisted forearm pronation with elbow extended. Associated with Ulnar Neuropathy in 20%. Management: Conservative in 90%: Rest, counterforce elbow strap, eccentric wrist physiotherapy, topical NSAIDs. Refractory: Platelet-Rich Plasma (PRP) or surgical debridement of degenerated ECRB tendon (Nirschl procedure).",
