              "rationale": "Pemphigus Vulgaris is an autoimmune blistering disorder characterized by IgG autoantibodies targeting desmoglein-3 (mucosal-predominant) and desmoglein-1 (mucocutaneous). Desmogleins are cadherin-type cell adhesion proteins within desmosomes. Autoantibody binding disrupts desmosomes, causing ACANTHOLYSIS (loss of intercellular adhesion between keratinocytes). Histopathology Hallmark: SUPRABASAL BLISTER with intraepidermal acantholysis, rounded free-floating Tzanck acantholytic cells, and the intact basal layer adhering to the basement membrane resembling a 'ROW OF TOMBSTONES'. Clinical Signs: Oral erosions precede skin blisters by months; Flaccid, fragile blisters; NIKOLSKY SIGN POSITIVE (shearing epidermis with lateral sliding pressure); Asboe-Hansen sign positive (bulla spread sign). Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF): Intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 in a 'FISHNET' / 'CHICKEN-WIRE' pattern. In contrast, Bullous Pemphigoid features tense subepidermal bullae, Nikolsky negative, and linear IgG/C3 along the basement membrane zone.",
