              "rationale": "Pityriasis Rosea (PR): An acute, self-limiting, benign exanthematous eruption of children and young adults (peak age 10 to 35 years). Etiology: Reactivation of HUMAN HERPESVIRUS-6 (HHV-6) and HUMAN HERPESVIRUS-7 (HHV-7). CLASSICAL CLINICAL EVOLUTION: (1) THE 'HERALD PATCH' (Mother Patch): Occurs in 80% of patients. A single, solitary, large (2 to 5 cm), round or oval, salmon-pink or fawn-colored plaque with a delicate, inward-facing fine 'COLLARETTE OF SCALE' (scale is attached peripherally and free medially, pointing inward!); typically appears on the trunk or proximal thighs 1 to 2 weeks BEFORE the generalized eruption (frequently misdiagnosed as tinea corporis!); (2) Secondary Generalized Exanthem: 1-2 weeks later, crops of hundreds of smaller (0.5-1.5 cm) oval salmon-colored macules and papules erupt across the trunk and proximal extremities; (3) PATHOGNOMONIC 'CHRISTMAS TREE' / 'FIR TREE' DISTRIBUTION: The oval lesions align symmetrically with their long axes parallel to the skin cleavage lines (Langer's lines), angling downward and outward from the spine like the branches of an evergreen fir tree. Pruritus is mild or absent. VITAL DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS: SECONDARY SYPHILIS (Secondary syphilis can present identically with a generalized papulosquamous eruption; ALWAYS EXAMINE PALMS AND SOLES and order VDRL / RPR serology: PR characteristically SPARES THE PALMS AND SOLES!). Natural History & Management: Self-limiting; spontaneous complete resolution occurs within 6 TO 8 WEEKS without scarring. Treatment is purely symptomatic (antihistamines, emollients, topical calamine; mild topical corticosteroids for itching).",
