              "rationale": "Rosacea: A chronic centrofacial inflammatory dermatosis characterized by vascular hyperreactivity, neurovascular dysregulation, and innate immune dysfunction (overactivation of Cathelicidin antimicrobial peptide LL-37 and kallikrein 5). Demographics: Adults aged 30 to 50 years, Celtic / fair skin types; female:male 3:1 (except rhinophyma which is 10:1 male). Trigger Factors: Thermal and vasodilator triggers: Hot beverages, spicy foods, alcohol, sunlight, heat, emotional stress, and local skin colonization by the follicular mite DEMODEX FOLLICULORUM. FOUR CLINICAL SUBTYPES: (1) Subtype 1: Erythematotelangiectatic Rosacea: Persistent central facial erythema, flushing episodes, and fine branching telangiectasias; (2) Subtype 2: Papulopustular Rosacea: Central facial erythema studded with small dome-shaped inflammatory papules and pustules. CRUCIAL DISTINGUISHING HALLMARK: COMEDONES ARE STRICTLY ABSENT IN ROSACEA (the presence of open or closed comedones excludes rosacea and confirms acne vulgaris!); (3) Subtype 3: Phymatous Rosacea: Marked hypertrophy of sebaceous glands, fibrous connective tissue hyperplasia, and nodular bulbous enlargement of the nose: RHINOPHYMA (predominantly affects older men; treated surgically or with CO2 laser ablation!); (4) Subtype 4: Ocular Rosacea (in > 50%): Blepharitis, conjunctival injection, gritty sensation, recurrent chalazia, and corneal ulceration. Pharmacotherapy: General: Avoid thermal triggers, daily broad-spectrum sunscreens; Topical: Topical Metronidazole (0.75% or 1%), Topical Ivermectin (kills Demodex mites!), or Azelaic acid 15%; Oral: Sub-antimicrobial low-dose Oral Doxycycline (40 mg modified-release daily; anti-inflammatory action by inhibiting matrix metalloproteinases).",
