            "question": "A 26-year-old male discovers an asymptomatic, completely smooth, circular, non-scarring bald patch on his scalp. Dermoscopy of the periphery of the patch reveals pathognomonic 'Exclamation-Point Hairs' (hairs with a fractured, dark, expanded distal tip and a thin, tapered, hypopigmented proximal base). Scalp biopsy demonstrates a dense peribulbar lymphocytic infiltrate resembling a 'Swarm of Bees'. What is the diagnosis, and what is the first-line therapy for limited patchy hair loss?",
