            "question": "A 50-year-old female presents with symmetric proximal muscle weakness (difficulty climbing stairs and combing hair) and a violaceous, purplish periorbital rash with periorbital edema ('Heliotrope Rash'). Physical examination of the hands reveals pathognomonic erythematous, violaceous, flat-topped papules and plaques overlying the dorsal aspect of the Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and Interphalangeal (PIP) joints ('Gottron's Papules'). What is the diagnosis, and why is comprehensive age-appropriate screening for internal visceral malignancies mandatory?",
