            "id": "DERM_PYQ_006",
            "question_type": "PYQ",
            "exam_source": "NEET PG 2024",
            "topic": "Papulosquamous Disorders - Psoriasis & Auspitz Sign",
            "question": "A 35-year-old male presents with sharply demarcated, erythematous plaques covered with thick, silvery-white micaceous scales over both extensor surfaces of his elbows and knees. When the overlying scales are systematically scraped away, minute pinpoint droplets of bleeding appear on the uncovered surface. What is the name of this pathognomonic clinical sign, and what underlying histopathological feature produces it?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Auspitz sign; Dilated, tortuous capillaries within thinned suprapapillary epidermal plates",
              "B": "Nikolsky sign; Suprabasal acantholytic split with intraepidermal blister formation",
              "C": "Koebner phenomenon; Deposition of immune complexes along the dermo-epidermal junction",
              "D": "Darier sign; Mast cell degranulation triggering localized urticarial wheals"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Psoriasis vulgaris is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory dermatosis driven by the IL-23/IL-17 axis, leading to marked epidermal hyperproliferation and keratinocyte turnover. Clinical diagnosis is supported by the Grattage test: 1. Candle grease sign (scales peel off like wax). 2. Last membrane sign (translucent Berkley's membrane). 3. AUSPITZ SIGN: Pinpoint bleeding occurring upon removal of the thin membrane, caused by rupture of dilated, tortuous, hyperpermeable capillary loops within elongated dermal papillae that are covered only by an extraordinarily thin suprapapillary epidermal plate. Other histopathologic features include parakeratosis, regular acanthosis ('test-tube in a rack' rete pegs), and neutrophils in the stratum corneum (Munro microabscesses).",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "Correct. Auspitz sign is caused by thinned suprapapillary plates exposing dilated dermal capillaries.",
                "B": "Nikolsky sign (sloughing of epidermis with lateral pressure) occurs in Pemphigus vulgaris and SJS/TEN.",
                "C": "Koebner phenomenon is the appearance of psoriatic lesions along lines of mechanical skin trauma.",
                "D": "Darier sign occurs in Cutaneous Mastocytosis (urticaria upon rubbing pigmented macule)."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Psoriasis Milestones: Auspitz sign = Pinpoint bleeding on scale removal (thinned suprapapillary plates + capillary tortuosity). Munro microabscesses = Neutrophils in stratum corneum. Spongiform pustules of Kogoj = Neutrophils in spinous layer. Koebner phenomenon positive. Nail changes: Pitting (most common), Oil drop / Salmon patch sign (most specific), Subungual hyperkeratosis."
            },
            "subject_id": "DERMATOLOGY",
            "subject_name": "Dermatology"
