              "rationale": "Erysipelas vs Cellulitis (Bacterial Skin & Soft Tissue Infections): (1) ERYSIPELAS: An acute, non-necrotizing superficial dermal infection. Causative Organism: STREPTOCOCCUS PYOGENES (Group A Beta-Hemolytic Streptococcus / GABHS in > 85-90% of cases!). Anatomical Level: Confined strictly to the UPPER DERMIS and prominent SUPERFICIAL DERMAL LYMPHATICS. Anatomical Sites: Lower legs (#1 site today, 80%) and FACE (malar cheeks and bridge of nose, 'Butterfly distribution' in 15-20%; Milian's Ear Sign positive: involvement of the ear pinna confirms erysipelas because the pinna lacks subcutaneous fat, so deeper cellulitis cannot occur there!). Clinical Hallmarks: Sudden explosive onset with high fever, rigors, and shaking chills; Plaque is BRIGHT FIERY RED, TENSE, INDURATED, PAINFUL, GLISTENING, with a SHARPLY DEMARCATED, ABRUPTLY ELEVATED ADVANCING MARGIN (the edge is easily palpable as a raised step-off!); superficial bullae can develop; (2) CELLULITIS: An acute bacterial infection extending into DEEPER layers: Involves the DEEP RETICULAR DERMIS and SUBCUTANEOUS FAT (adipose tissue). Causative Organisms: Streptococcus pyogenes and STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS. Clinical Hallmarks: Indolent onset over days; Erythematous plaque is duller pinkish-red, warm, tender, with FLAT, ILL-DEFINED, VAGUE, INDISTINCT MARGINS that merge imperceptibly into normal surrounding skin (no raised step-off border); localized crepitus or purulence indicates S. aureus or anaerobes. Risk Factors for Both: Venous stasis, lymphedema (post-mastectomy), tinea pedis (cracked interdigital portal of entry), and obesity. First-Line Medical Therapy: Mild / Outpatient Erysipelas = Oral Penicillin V or Amoxicillin; Severe / Inpatient = IV Penicillin G or IV Cefazolin.",
