            "question": "A 52-year-old diabetic male presents with rapidly progressive swelling, erythema, and excruciating pain in his right perineum and thigh following a perianal abscess. Within 24 hours, the pain becomes uncharacteristically dull and numb (anesthetic skin), with skin turning dusky violaceous with bullae containing foul-smelling 'Dishwater' fluid and soft-tissue crepitus. Laboratory Risk Indicator for Necrotizing Fasciitis (LRINEC) score is 8 (high risk >= 6). What are the two microbiological types, and what is the definitive life-saving management?",
