              "rationale": "Cutaneous Leishmaniasis (Old World Cutaneous Leishmaniasis / 'Oriental Sore' / 'Delhi Boil' / 'Aleppo Boil'): Caused by the obligate intracellular protozoan parasite LEISHMANIA TROPICA (urban anthroponotic) and LEISHMANIA MAJOR (rural zoonotic; rodent reservoir) in the Middle East, Mediterranean, Central Asia, and Northwest India (Rajasthan). Transmission Vector: Bite of the infected female SANDFLY of the genus PHLEBOTOMUS (transmits flagellated, motile PROMASTIGOTES into the dermis during blood feeding). Pathophysiology: Promastigotes are phagocytosed by dermal macrophages, where they transform into non-flagellated, non-motile AMASTIGOTES that multiply within phagolysosomes until the macrophage ruptures. CLINICAL HALLMARKS: Occurs on exposed body parts (forearms, face, legs): (1) Begins as an asymptomatic, firm red papule 2 to 8 weeks after sandfly bite; (2) Gradually enlarges into a dusky, painless nodule that undergoes central ischemic necrosis; (3) Forms a characteristic CHRONIC, CRATER-LIKE, INDURATED, PAINLESS ULCER WITH A FIRM, RAISED, ROLLED, VIOLACEOUS BORDER and a central adherent hyperkeratotic crust ('Volcano-Like' ulcer / 'Delhi Boil'); (4) Spontaneous healing occurs very slowly over 1 to 2 years, leaving an unsightly, cribriform, hyperpigmented, depressed SCAR. DIAGNOSTIC GOLD STANDARD: Giemsa-stained slit-skin smear or punch biopsy: PATHOGNOMONIC LEISHMAN-DONOVAN (LD) BODIES: Round to oval amastigotes (2 to 4 um in diameter) packed inside the cytoplasm of histiocytes/macrophages, featuring a prominent spherical nucleus and a rod-shaped bar-like KINETOPLAST perpendicular to the nucleus. Culture on Novy-MacNeal-Nicolle (NNN) medium yields motile promastigotes. TREATMENT: (1) Local: Intralesional Sodium Stibogluconate injection or Cryotherapy for small solitary lesions; (2) Systemic: PENTAVALENT ANTIMONIALS (Intramuscular/IV Sodium Stibogluconate / Meglumine Antimoniate at 20 mg/kg/day for 20 days) or Intravenous LIPOSOMAL AMPHOTERICIN B (drug of choice for antimonial-resistant strains). Oral Miltefosine is also highly effective.",
