            "question": "A 42-year-old male presents with a slowly spreading, chronic, solitary, reddish-brown plaque on his cheek that has enlarged centrifugally over several years. Diascopy (pressing a glass microscope slide firmly against the lesion) blenches the background erythema, revealing distinctive, translucent, yellowish-brown nodular spots resembling 'Apple-Jelly Nodules'. What is the most common form of reinfection Cutaneous Tuberculosis, and what histopathology is characteristic?",
