              "rationale": "Cutaneous Tuberculosis: Manifestations of Mycobacterium tuberculosis infection in the skin, categorized by host immunity and route of entry: (1) LUPUS VULGARIS: The MOST COMMON FORM OF CUTANEOUS TUBERCULOSIS WORLDWIDE in patients with MODERATE-TO-HIGH CELL-MEDIATED IMMUNITY (post-primary endogenous reinfection via hematogenous, lymphatic, or direct extension from an underlying focus). Anatomical Site: Over 80% occur on the FACE AND NECK (nose, cheeks, earlobes; classically in cold damp climates). Clinical Hallmarks: Slowly progressive, destructive, reddish-brown or gelatinous soft plaques that spread centrifugally with central scarring and atrophy. PATHOGNOMONIC CLINICAL SIGN: 'APPLE-JELLY' SIGN ON DIASCOPY: Firm pressure with a clear glass slide empties congested capillaries (dermal blanching), unmasking underlying tuberculoid granulomas which appear as translucent, yellowish-brown, gelatinous 'apple-jelly' macules/nodules! Complication: Destruction of underlying nasal and auricular cartilage (mutilating deformity); MARJOLIN'S ULCER (Squamous cell carcinoma develops in chronic long-standing lupus vulgaris scars in 1-2%!). Histopathology: Well-formed epithelioid cell tubercles with Langhans giant cells and minimal caseous necrosis (paucibacillary, AFB rarely seen on smear; PCR is positive); (2) SCROFULODERMA (Tuberculosis Colliquativa Cutis): Most common form in children in India: Direct extension of infection from an underlying tuberculous focus (cervical lymph nodes, bone, joint) to the overlying skin, forming cold subcutaneous nodules that break down into chronic discharging sinuses and puckered cord-like scars; (3) Tuberculosis Verrucosa Cutis (Prosector's Wart): Exogenous inoculation of M. tuberculosis into skin of a previously sensitized immune individual (pathologists, butchers, farmers: warty hyperkeratotic plaque on hands); (4) Tuberculous Chancre: Exogenous inoculation into a non-immune individual. Treatment: Full standard 6-month Antitubercular Therapy (ATT: 2HRZE + 4HR).",
