              "rationale": "Secondary Syphilis (The 'Great Imitator'): Manifests 4 TO 10 WEEKS after the initial primary hard chancre has healed, caused by widespread hematogenous and lymphatic dissemination of Treponema pallidum. Highly contagious! CARDINAL CLINICAL HALLMARKS: (1) SYPHILITIC EXANTHEM (SYPHILIDE): Symmetrical, non-pruritic, generalized maculopapular, lenticular, or papulosquamous eruption. Color: Classically described as 'COPPER-PENNY' or 'RAW-HAM' colored. PATHOGNOMONIC PREDILECTION: INVOLVES THE PALMS OF HANDS AND SOLES OF FEET (a macular or scaly papular rash on palms and soles in a young sexually active adult is SECONDARY SYPHILIS until proven otherwise!); surrounded by a delicate ring of scale called BIETT'S COLLARETTE; (2) CONDYLOMATA LATA (in 10-20%): Highly infectious, broad, flat, moist, greyish-white or flesh-colored vegetative hypertrophic papules and plaques occurring in warm, moist, intertriginous skin: PERIANAL REGION, vulva, scrotum, and inner thighs (Darkfield microscopy of condyloma exudate swarms with motile spirochetes!); (3) MUCOUS PATCHES / SNAIL-TRACK ULCERS: Painless, shallow, oval, greyish-white superficial mucosal erosions on the lips, buccal mucosa, tongue, or soft palate, resembling the shiny trail of a snail (highly infectious!); (4) GENERALIZED LYMPHADENOPATHY (in 85%): Painless, firm, discrete, rubbery nodes; PALPABLE EPITROCHLEAR LYMPHADENOPATHY IS HIGHLY SUGGESTIVE OF SECONDARY SYPHILIS; (5) 'Moth-Eaten' Alopecia (patchy, irregular, non-scarring hair loss on scalp and eyebrows). DIAGNOSTIC SEROLOGY: Non-Treponemal Tests (VDRL and RPR) are 100% POSITIVE with high titers (e.g. >= 1:32; note: rare false-negative Prozone Phenomenon can occur due to antibody excess!); confirmed by Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TPHA). TREATMENT OF CHOICE: BENZATHINE PENICILLIN G: 2.4 MILLION UNITS IM AS A SINGLE DOSE. Jarisch-Herxheimer Reaction: Acute febrile reaction (fever, chills, flushing, worsening of rash) occurring within 2 to 12 hours of penicillin administration due to massive endotoxin-like spirochetal lysis; self-limiting, managed with antipyretics.",
