            "topic": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) Management",
            "question": "A 28-year-old male spends 4 hours every day repeatedly washing his hands because of distressing intrusive thoughts that he will contaminate his family. He recognizes that these thoughts are irrational. What is the first-line behavioral therapy and pharmacological treatment for OCD?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) + High-dose SSRI",
              "B": "Systematic desensitization + Low-dose Haloperidol",
              "C": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) + Lithium",
              "D": "Supportive psychotherapy + Buspirone"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "The gold-standard psychotherapy for OCD is Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP), a specialized form of CBT where the patient is exposed to the obsession and restrained from performing the compulsion. Pharmacotherapy of choice is high-dose SSRI (e.g. Fluoxetine, Sertraline, Fluvoxamine) or Clomipramine (TCA).",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "CORRECT: The gold-standard psychotherapy for OCD is Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP), a specialized form of CBT where the patient is exposed to the obsession and restrained from performing the compulsion. Pharmacotherapy of choice is high-dose SSRI (e.g. Fluoxetine, Sertraline, Fluvoxamine) or Clomipramine (TCA).",
                "B": "INCORRECT: Systematic desensitization + Low-dose Haloperidol is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "C": "INCORRECT: Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) + Lithium is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "D": "INCORRECT: Supportive psychotherapy + Buspirone is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "OCD: Obsessions (intrusive, ego-dystonic thoughts) + Compulsions (repetitive behaviors relieving anxiety). 1st-line: High-dose SSRI + Exposure & Response Prevention (ERP). Clomipramine is most serotonergic TCA."
            },
