            "topic": "Tourette Syndrome Diagnosis & Therapy",
            "question": "An 11-year-old boy presents with repetitive rapid eye blinking, shoulder shrugging, and throat clearing, alongside involuntary vocal grunting and occasional coprolalia for 18 months. What is the diagnosis?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Tourette Syndrome",
              "B": "Chronic motor tic disorder",
              "C": "Sydenham chorea",
              "D": "PANDAS"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Tourette Syndrome requires BOTH multiple motor tics AND at least one vocal tic present for more than 1 year, with onset before age 18. Treatment includes Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT), alpha-2 agonists (Guanfacine, Clonidine), or dopamine antagonists.",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "CORRECT: Tourette Syndrome requires BOTH multiple motor tics AND at least one vocal tic present for more than 1 year, with onset before age 18. Treatment includes Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics (CBIT), alpha-2 agonists (Guanfacine, Clonidine), or dopamine antagonists.",
                "B": "INCORRECT: Chronic motor tic disorder is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "C": "INCORRECT: Sydenham chorea is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "D": "INCORRECT: PANDAS is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Tourette Syndrome: Multiple motor tics + >= 1 vocal tic for > 1 year before age 18. Strongly comorbid with ADHD and OCD. Treatment: CBIT, Alpha-2 agonists (Guanfacine/Clonidine), Atypical antipsychotics (Aripiprazole)."
            },
