              "rationale": "Local anesthetics consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring, an intermediate chain (ester or amide), and a hydrophilic tertiary amine. Esters (Procaine, Tetracaine, Cocaine, Benzocaine) contain an ester linkage, are rapidly hydrolyzed in plasma by Pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase), and yield Para-Aminobenzoic Acid (PABA), which can induce true IgE-mediated allergic reactions. Amides (Lidocaine, Bupivacaine, Ropivacaine, Mepivacaine, Prilocaine) contain an amide linkage, are metabolized by hepatic cytochrome P450, and rarely cause allergic reactions. Eponym rule: Amides have two 'i's in their name (L-i-doca-i-ne, Bup-i-vaca-i-ne); Esters have only one 'i' (Proca-i-ne).",
