            "question": "A 68-year-old male with severe COPD and chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure (baseline PaCO2 55 mmHg) requires supplemental oxygen. Which oxygen delivery device is the device of choice because it provides a fixed, precise, guaranteed fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO2) independent of the patient's inspiratory flow rate or breathing pattern?",
            "options": {
              "A": "Venturi Mask (High-flow air-entrainment mask utilizing Bernoulli's principle)",
              "B": "Nasal Cannula at 4 L/min",
              "C": "Simple Face Mask at 6 L/min",
              "D": "Non-Rebreather Face Mask with reservoir bag at 15 L/min"
            },
            "correct_option": "A",
            "explanation": {
              "rationale": "Oxygen devices are classified into Variable Performance (low-flow: nasal cannula, simple face mask, non-rebreather mask) where delivered FiO2 fluctuates widely with the patient's tidal volume and inspiratory flow rate, and Fixed Performance (high-flow). The VENTURI MASK (air-entrainment mask) is a fixed-performance system that utilizes Bernoulli's Principle: high-velocity oxygen passing through a narrow constriction creates a localized pressure drop that entrains a constant, precise volume of ambient room air, delivering a guaranteed, exact FiO2 (24%, 28%, 31%, 35%, 40%, or 50%). It is the undisputed device of choice in COPD patients susceptible to loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive and CO2 retention.",
              "distractor_analysis": {
                "A": "CORRECT: Oxygen devices are classified into Variable Performance (low-flow: nasal cannula, simple face mask, non-rebreather mask) where delivered FiO2 fluctuates widely with the patient's tidal volume and inspiratory flow rate, and Fixed Performance (high-flow). The VENTURI MASK (air-entrainment mask) is a fixed-performance system that utilizes Bernoulli's Principle: high-velocity oxygen passing through a narrow constriction creates a localized pressure drop that entrains a constant, precise volume of ambient room air, delivering a guaranteed, exact FiO2 (24%, 28%, 31%, 35%, 40%, or 50%). It is the undisputed device of choice in COPD patients susceptible to loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive and CO2 retention.",
                "B": "INCORRECT: Nasal Cannula at 4 L/min is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "C": "INCORRECT: Simple Face Mask at 6 L/min is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario.",
                "D": "INCORRECT: Non-Rebreather Face Mask with reservoir bag at 15 L/min is not the primary mechanism, culprit, or first-line choice in this clinical scenario."
              },
              "high_yield_pearl": "Oxygen Delivery Comparison: Low-Flow (Variable FiO2): Nasal Cannula (1-6 L/min, FiO2 24-44%, adds ~4% per L/min); Simple Face Mask (5-10 L/min, FiO2 40-60%); Non-Rebreather Mask with reservoir (10-15 L/min, FiO2 80-90%). High-Flow (Fixed FiO2): VENTURI MASK (delivers exact FiO2 24-50% via Bernoulli principle / jet entrainment; essential in COPD to prevent oxygen-induced hypercapnia)."
            },
